of_Donald_Trump \\\"Presidential eligibility of Donald Trump\\\")\\n    * [Agenda 47](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agenda_47 \\\"Agenda 47\\\")\\n    * [rallies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_rallies_for_the_2024_Donald_Trump_presidential_campaign \\\"List of rallies for the 2024 Donald Trump presidential campaign\\\")\\n    * [convention](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Republican_National_Convention \\\"2024 Republican National Convention\\\")\\n    * [debates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_presidential_debates \\\"2024 United States presidential debates\\\")\\n    * assassination attempts \\n      * [PA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempted_assassination_of_Donald_Trump_in_Pennsylvania \\\"Attempted assassination of Donald Trump in Pennsylvania\\\")\\n        * [perpetrator](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Matthew_Crooks \\\"Thomas Matthew Crooks\\\")\\n        * [photographs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_raised-fist_photographs \\\"Donald Trump raised-fist photographs\\\")\\n      * [FL](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempted_assassination_of_Donald_Trump_in_Florida \\\"Attempted assassination of Donald Trump in Florida\\\")\\n        * [suspect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryan_Wesley_Routh \\\"Ryan Wesley Routh\\\")\\n\\n\\n* * *\\nImpeachments\\n  * [Efforts to impeach Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Efforts_to_impeach_Donald_Trump \\\"Efforts to impeach Donald Trump\\\")\\n    * [resolutions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_impeachment_resolutions_introduced_against_Donald_Trump \\\"List of impeachment resolutions introduced against Donald Trump\\\")\\n  * [First impeachment](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump \\\"First impeachment of Donald Trump\\\")\\n    * [Zelenskyy phone call](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019_Trump%E2%80%93Zelenskyy_phone_call \\\"2019 Trump–Zelenskyy phone call\\\")\\n    * [Ukraine scandal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019_Trump%E2%80%93Ukraine_scandal \\\"2019 Trump–Ukraine scandal\\\")\\n    * [House inquiry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impeachment_inquiry_into_Donald_Trump \\\"Impeachment inquiry into Donald Trump\\\")\\n    * [Senate trial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_impeachment_trial_of_Donald_Trump \\\"First impeachment trial of Donald Trump\\\")\\n  * [Second impeachment](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Second impeachment of Donald Trump\\\")\\n    * [Capitol attack](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_6_United_States_Capitol_attack \\\"January 6 United States Capitol attack\\\")\\n    * [Senate trial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_impeachment_trial_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Second impeachment trial of Donald Trump\\\")\\n  * [Proposed expungements](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proposed_expungements_of_the_impeachments_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Proposed expungements of the impeachments of Donald Trump\\\")\\n\\n\\n* * *\\n[Legal proceedings](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_and_business_legal_affairs_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump\\\")\\n  * [2020 election federal indictment](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_\\\\(election_obstruction_case\\\\) \\\"Federal prosecution of Donald Trump \\\\(election obstruction case\\\\)\\\")\\n  * [Classified documents federal indictment](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_\\\\(classified_documents_case\\\\) \\\"Federal prosecution of Donald Trump \\\\(classified documents case\\\\)\\\")\\n    * [Special counsel investigation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smith_special_counsel_investigation \\\"Smith special counsel investigation\\\")\\n    * [FBI investigation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FBI_investigation_into_Donald_Trump%27s_handling_of_government_documents \\\"FBI investigation into Donald Trump's handling of government documents\\\")\\n    * [FBI search of Mar-a-Lago](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FBI_search_of_Mar-a-Lago \\\"FBI search of Mar-a-Lago\\\")\\n    * _[Trump v. United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_v._United_States_\\\\(2022\\\\) \\\"Trump v. United States \\\\(2022\\\\)\\\")_\\n  * [New York felony conviction for falsifying business records](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_in_New_York \\\"Prosecution of Donald Trump in New York\\\")\\n    * [Stormy Daniels scandal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stormy_Daniels%E2%80%93Donald_Trump_scandal \\\"Stormy Daniels–Donald Trump scandal\\\")\\n  * [New York investigations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_investigations_of_the_Trump_Organization \\\"New York investigations of the Trump Organization\\\")\\n  * [Georgia election indictment](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_election_racketeering_prosecution \\\"Georgia election racketeering prosecution\\\")\\n    * [Georgia election investigation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_Georgia_election_investigation \\\"2020 Georgia election investigation\\\")\\n    * [mug shot](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mug_shot_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Mug shot of Donald Trump\\\")\\n\\n\\n* * *\\n  \\n  * [v](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Donald_Trump_series \\\"Template:Donald Trump series\\\")\\n  * [t](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Donald_Trump_series \\\"Template talk:Donald Trump series\\\")\\n  * [e](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Template:Donald_Trump_series \\\"Special:EditPage/Template:Donald Trump series\\\")\\n\\n  \\n**Donald John Trump** (born June 14, 1946) is an American politician, media personality, and businessman who is the 47th [president of the United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_the_United_States \\\"President of the United States\\\"). A member of the [Republican Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republican_Party_\\\\(United_States\\\\) \\\"Republican Party \\\\(United States\\\\)\\\"), he served as the 45th president from 2017 to 2021. \\nBorn into a wealthy family in New York City, Trump graduated from the [University of Pennsylvania](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Pennsylvania \\\"University of Pennsylvania\\\") in 1968 with a [bachelor's degree](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bachelor%27s_degree \\\"Bachelor's degree\\\") in economics. He became the president of his family's real estate business in 1971, renamed it [the Trump Organization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Trump_Organization \\\"The Trump Organization\\\"), and began acquiring and building skyscrapers, hotels, casinos, and golf courses. He launched side ventures, many licensing the Trump name, and filed for six business bankruptcies in the 1990s and 2000s. From 2004 to 2015, he hosted the reality television show _[The Apprentice](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Apprentice_\\\\(American_TV_series\\\\) \\\"The Apprentice \\\\(American TV series\\\\)\\\")_ , bolstering his fame as a billionaire. Presenting himself as a political outsider, Trump won the [2016 presidential election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_United_States_presidential_election \\\"2016 United States presidential election\\\") against [Democratic Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Party_\\\\(United_States\\\\) \\\"Democratic Party \\\\(United States\\\\)\\\") nominee [Hillary Clinton](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hillary_Clinton \\\"Hillary Clinton\\\"). \\nDuring his first presidency, Trump imposed [a travel ban](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_13769 \\\"Executive Order 13769\\\") on seven Muslim-majority countries, expanded the [Mexico–United States border wall](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico%E2%80%93United_States_border_wall \\\"Mexico–United States border wall\\\"), and enforced [a family separation policy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_administration_family_separation_policy \\\"Trump administration family separation policy\\\") on the border. He rolled back environmental and business regulations, signed the [Tax Cuts and Jobs Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_Cuts_and_Jobs_Act \\\"Tax Cuts and Jobs Act\\\"), and appointed three Supreme Court justices. In foreign policy, Trump withdrew the U.S. from agreements on climate, trade, and Iran's nuclear program, and initiated [a trade war with China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93United_States_trade_war \\\"China–United States trade war\\\"). In response to the [COVID-19 pandemic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic_in_the_United_States \\\"COVID-19 pandemic in the United States\\\") from 2020, he downplayed its severity, contradicted health officials, and signed the [CARES Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CARES_Act \\\"CARES Act\\\"). After losing the [2020 presidential election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_presidential_election \\\"2020 United States presidential election\\\") to [Joe Biden](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Biden \\\"Joe Biden\\\"), Trump [attempted to overturn the result](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempts_to_overturn_the_2020_United_States_presidential_election \\\"Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election\\\"), culminating in the [January 6 Capitol attack](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_6_Capitol_attack \\\"January 6 Capitol attack\\\") in 2021. He was impeached in 2019 for abuse of power and obstruction of Congress, and in 2021 for incitement of insurrection; the Senate acquitted him both times. \\nIn 2023, Trump was found liable in civil cases [for sexual abuse and defamation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E._Jean_Carroll_v._Donald_J._Trump \\\"E. Jean Carroll v. Donald J. Trump\\\") and [for business fraud](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_business_fraud_lawsuit_against_the_Trump_Organization \\\"New York business fraud lawsuit against the Trump Organization\\\"). He was found guilty [of falsifying business records](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_in_New_York \\\"Prosecution of Donald Trump in New York\\\") in 2024, making him the first U.S. president convicted of a felony. After winning the [2024 presidential election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_presidential_election \\\"2024 United States presidential election\\\") against [Kamala Harris](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamala_Harris \\\"Kamala Harris\\\"), he was sentenced to a penalty-free [discharge](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discharge_\\\\(sentence\\\\) \\\"Discharge \\\\(sentence\\\\)\\\"), and two felony indictments against him for [retention of classified documents](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_\\\\(classified_documents_case\\\\) \\\"Federal prosecution of Donald Trump \\\\(classified documents case\\\\)\\\") and [obstruction of the 2020 election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_\\\\(election_obstruction_case\\\\) \\\"Federal prosecution of Donald Trump \\\\(election obstruction case\\\\)\\\") were dismissed without prejudice. [A racketeering case](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_election_racketeering_prosecution \\\"Georgia election racketeering prosecution\\\") related to the 2020 election in Georgia is pending. \\nTrump began his second presidency by initiating [mass layoffs of federal workers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_United_States_federal_mass_layoffs \\\"2025 United States federal mass layoffs\\\"). He [imposed tariffs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariffs_in_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Tariffs in the second Trump administration\\\") on nearly all countries at the highest level since the [Great Depression](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Depression \\\"Great Depression\\\") and signed the [One Big Beautiful Bill Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_Big_Beautiful_Bill_Act \\\"One Big Beautiful Bill Act\\\"). His administration's actions—including [intimidation of political opponents and civil society](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Targeting_of_political_opponents_and_civil_society_under_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Targeting of political opponents and civil society under the second Trump administration\\\"), deportations of immigrants, and extensive use of executive orders—have drawn over 300 [lawsuits challenging their legality](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legal_affairs_of_the_second_Donald_Trump_presidency \\\"Legal affairs of the second Donald Trump presidency\\\"). High-profile cases have underscored his broad interpretation of the [unitary executive theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unitary_executive_theory \\\"Unitary executive theory\\\") and have led to significant conflicts with the federal courts. Judges found many of his administration's actions to be illegal, and several have been described as unconstitutional. \\nSince 2015, Trump's leadership style and political agenda—often referred to as [Trumpism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trumpism \\\"Trumpism\\\")—have reshaped the Republican Party's identity. Many of his comments and actions have been [characterized as racist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racial_views_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Racial views of Donald Trump\\\") or misogynistic, and he has [made false or misleading statements](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/False_or_misleading_statements_by_Donald_Trump \\\"False or misleading statements by Donald Trump\\\") and [promoted conspiracy theories](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conspiracy_theories_promoted_by_Donald_Trump \\\"List of conspiracy theories promoted by Donald Trump\\\") to an extent unprecedented in American politics. Trump's actions, especially in his second term, have been described as authoritarian and contributing to [democratic backsliding](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_backsliding_in_the_United_States \\\"Democratic backsliding in the United States\\\"). After his first term, scholars and historians [ranked him](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historical_rankings_of_presidents_of_the_United_States#Scholar_survey_summary \\\"Historical rankings of presidents of the United States\\\") as one of the worst presidents in American history. \\n## Early life and education\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_NYMA.jpg)At [New York Military Academy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Military_Academy \\\"New York Military Academy\\\"), 1964\\nDonald John Trump was born on June 14, 1946, at [Jamaica Hospital](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamaica_Hospital \\\"Jamaica Hospital\\\") in the New York City borough of [Queens](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queens \\\"Queens\\\"), the fourth child of [Fred Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Trump \\\"Fred Trump\\\") and [Mary Anne MacLeod Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Anne_MacLeod_Trump \\\"Mary Anne MacLeod Trump\\\").[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201730,_37-1) He is of German and Scottish descent.[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017v-2) He grew up with his older siblings, [Maryanne](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maryanne_Trump_Barry \\\"Maryanne Trump Barry\\\"), [Fred Jr.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Trump_Jr. \\\"Fred Trump Jr.\\\"), and Elizabeth, and his younger brother, [Robert](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Trump \\\"Robert Trump\\\"), in a mansion in the [Jamaica Estates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamaica_Estates \\\"Jamaica Estates\\\") neighborhood of Queens.[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-3) Fred Trump paid his children each about $20,000 a year, equivalent to $265,000 a year in 2024. Trump was a millionaire in inflation-adjusted dollars by age eight.[[4]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202430%E2%80%9331-4)[[a]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-5)\\nTrump attended the private [Kew-Forest School](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kew-Forest_School \\\"Kew-Forest School\\\") through seventh grade. He was a difficult child and showed an early interest in his father's business. His father enrolled him in [New York Military Academy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Military_Academy \\\"New York Military Academy\\\"), a private boarding school, to complete secondary school.[[5]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201733,_38,_45-6) The academy pushed students into sports[[6]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202466-7) and taught the imperative of winning.[[7]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201742-8) In high school his grades improved to a B average.[[8]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201673-9) [[b]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-11)\\nTrump considered a show business career but instead, to be closer to home, enrolled at [Fordham University](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fordham_University \\\"Fordham University\\\") in 1964.[[10]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201745%E2%80%9347-12) He participated in the [Reserve Officers' Training Corps](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reserve_Officers%27_Training_Corps \\\"Reserve Officers' Training Corps\\\") program during his first year, attending classes in a military uniform every Wednesday, but dropped it in his second year.[[11]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202499-13) He dropped football after three or four weeks and was a mediocre squash and tennis player.[[12]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201674-14) His Fordham friends introduced him to golf.[[13]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202498-15) His junior year he transferred to the [Wharton School](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wharton_School \\\"Wharton School\\\") of the [University of Pennsylvania](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Pennsylvania \\\"University of Pennsylvania\\\"), most often commuting to his father's office on weekends, and graduating in May 1968 with a [Bachelor of Science](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bachelor_of_Science \\\"Bachelor of Science\\\") in economics.[[14]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201747%E2%80%9350-16)[[15]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-UPenn-17) In college[[16]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675-18) he was not the top student he sometimes claimed to be.[[17]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201747-19) By the time he went to Wharton—where he does not appear in a list of those receiving honors[[c]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-20)—he was eyeing a career in real estate.[[16]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675-18) He was exempted from the draft during the Vietnam War due to a claim of [bone spurs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bone_spurs \\\"Bone spurs\\\") in his heels.[[18]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201748-21) Notwithstanding five years in military school,[[16]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675-18) he had no interest in going to war.[[18]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201748-21)\\nGrowing up, he considered his father and the family's pastor, [Norman Vincent Peale](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norman_Vincent_Peale \\\"Norman Vincent Peale\\\"),[[19]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a48-22) to be his mentors.[[20]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201781-23) His father told him repeatedly that he was \\\"a king\\\" and to be \\\"a killer\\\".[[21]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHurt_III199313-24) Peale preached self-confidence as the impetus for prosperity.[[22]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHaberman20229-25)[[d]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-27)\\n## Business career\\nMain articles: [Business career of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business_career_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Business career of Donald Trump\\\") and [The Trump Organization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Trump_Organization \\\"The Trump Organization\\\")\\nFurther information: [Business projects of Donald Trump in Russia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business_projects_of_Donald_Trump_in_Russia \\\"Business projects of Donald Trump in Russia\\\") and [Tax returns of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_returns_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Tax returns of Donald Trump\\\")\\n### Real estate\\nStarting in 1968, Trump was employed at Trump Management, his father's real estate company,[[24]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201750%E2%80%9351-28) which managed the middle-class apartment complexes Fred had built in [Queens](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queens \\\"Queens\\\"), [Staten Island](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staten_Island \\\"Staten Island\\\"), and [Brooklyn](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brooklyn \\\"Brooklyn\\\").[[25]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201752%E2%80%9353-29) His main tasks were collecting rent and making repairs[[26]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201676-30) for about five years.[[27]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a51-31) Captivated by its glamor and riches,[[28]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201758-32) Trump asked his father to expand to [Manhattan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan \\\"Manhattan\\\") where prices were at least three [orders of magnitude](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orders_of_magnitude \\\"Orders of magnitude\\\") higher, but his father was content in the outer boroughs.[[27]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2005a51-31) In 1971, he moved to Manhattan where he planned to move the business[[29]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201679-33) and commuted to his father's office.[[30]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201759-34) That year, his father made himself chairman and Trump president, overseeing 48 private corporations and 15 family partnerships.[[16]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201675-18) Trump began using [Trump Organization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Organization \\\"Trump Organization\\\") as an umbrella for the corporate names of his father's businesses.[[31]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBlair2015250-35)\\n[Roy Cohn](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roy_Cohn \\\"Roy Cohn\\\"), Trump's most important early influence after his father,[[32]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201681-36) was his [fixer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fixer_\\\\(person\\\\) \\\"Fixer \\\\(person\\\\)\\\"), lawyer, and mentor[[33]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett2016126-37) for 13 years in the 1970s and 1980s.[[34]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Mahler2016Cohn-38) Cohn taught Trump to think that life is transactional.[[35]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHaberman202233-39) In 1973, Cohn helped Trump countersue the U.S. government for $100 million (equivalent to $708 million in 2024[[36]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-inflation-US-40)) over its charges that Trump's properties had discriminated against Black applicants and tenants. Trump's counterclaims were dismissed, and the government's case was settled with the Trumps signing a consent decree agreeing to desegregate. Four years later, the Trumps again faced the courts when they were found in contempt of the decree.[[37]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201682%E2%80%9384-41) Helping Trump projects,[[38]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett2016190%E2%80%93191-42) Cohn was a [consigliere](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consigliere \\\"Consigliere\\\") whose Mafia connections controlled construction unions.[[39]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnston201645%E2%80%9346-43) In 1979, Cohn introduced political consultant [Roger Stone](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger_Stone \\\"Roger Stone\\\") to Trump, who enlisted Stone's services to deal with the federal government.[[40]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-44)\\nTrump moved from his studio to a penthouse with a view and got a real estate broker's license in the mid-1970s.[[41]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBarrett201678%E2%80%9380,_84-45) Before age thirty, he showed his propensity for litigation, no matter the outcome and cost; even when he lost, he described the case as a win.[[42]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024126-46) Over three decades as of 2018, Trump had been involved in more than 4,000 lawsuits,[[43]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-47) liens, and other filings, often filed for nonpayment against him by employees, contractors, real estate brokers, and his own attorneys.[[44]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-48) Between 1991 and 2009, Trump filed for [Chapter 11](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapter_11 \\\"Chapter 11\\\") bankruptcy protection for six of his businesses: the [Plaza Hotel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plaza_Hotel \\\"Plaza Hotel\\\") in Manhattan, the casinos in [Atlantic City, New Jersey](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_City,_New_Jersey \\\"Atlantic City, New Jersey\\\"), and the [Trump Hotels & Casino Resorts](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Hotels_%26_Casino_Resorts \\\"Trump Hotels & Casino Resorts\\\") company.[[45]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-49)[[46]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-TW-50)\\nIn 1992, Trump, his siblings Maryanne, Elizabeth, and Robert, and his cousin [John W. Walter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_W._Walter \\\"John W. Walter\\\") formed All County Building Supply & Maintenance Corp, each with a 20 percent share. The company had no offices and is alleged to have been a shell company for paying the vendors providing services and supplies for Trump's rental units, then billing those services and supplies to Trump Management with markups of 20–50 percent and more. The owners shared the proceeds generated by the markups. The increased costs were used to get state approval for increasing the rents of his rent-stabilized units. In January 1994, the siblings formed Apartment Management Associates and took over the management fees formerly collected by Trump Management. As well as inflating rents, the schemes served to transfer assets from Fred Trump to his children and nephew and lower the tax burden.[[47]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Tax_Schemes-51)\\n#### Manhattan and Chicago developments\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_with_model_of_Television_City.jpg)In 1985 with a model of one of his aborted Manhattan development projects[[48]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-52)\\nTrump attracted public attention in 1978 with the launch of his family's first Manhattan venture: the renovation of the derelict [Commodore Hotel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Hyatt_New_York \\\"Grand Hyatt New York\\\"), adjacent to Grand Central Terminal.[[49]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-53) The financing was facilitated by a $400 million city property tax abatement arranged for him by his father who also, jointly with [Hyatt](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyatt \\\"Hyatt\\\"), guaranteed a $70 million bank construction loan.[[50]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Rich_NYMag-54)[[51]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-55) The hotel reopened in 1980 as the [Grand Hyatt Hotel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Hyatt_New_York \\\"Grand Hyatt New York\\\"),[[52]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017\\\\[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA84_84\\\\]-56) and that same year, he obtained rights to develop [Trump Tower](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Tower \\\"Trump Tower\\\"), a mixed-use skyscraper in Midtown Manhattan.[[53]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-57) The building houses the headquarters of the Trump Corporation and Trump's [PAC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_action_committee \\\"Political action committee\\\") and was his primary residence until 2019.[[54]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-moved-58) In 1988, Trump acquired the Plaza Hotel with a loan from a consortium of 16 banks.[[55]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-59) The hotel filed for bankruptcy protection in 1992, and a reorganization plan was approved a month later, with the banks taking control of the property.[[56]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-60)\\nIn 1995, Trump defaulted on over $3 billion of bank loans, and the lenders seized the Plaza Hotel along with most of his other properties in a \\\"vast and humiliating restructuring\\\" that allowed him to avoid personal bankruptcy.[[57]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-plaza-61)[[58]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-62) The lead bank's attorney said of the banks' decision that they \\\"all agreed that he'd be better alive than dead\\\".[[57]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-plaza-61) In 1996, Trump acquired and renovated the mostly vacant 71-story skyscraper at [40 Wall Street](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/40_Wall_Street \\\"40 Wall Street\\\"), later rebranded as the Trump Building.[[59]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017\\\\[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidLqf0CwAAQBAJpgPA298_298\\\\]-63) In the early 1990s, he won the right to develop a 70-acre (28 ha) tract in the [Lincoln Square](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lincoln_Square,_Manhattan \\\"Lincoln Square, Manhattan\\\") neighborhood near the Hudson River. Struggling with debt from other ventures in 1994, he sold most of his interest in the project to Asian investors, who financed the project's completion, [Riverside South](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riverside_South,_Manhattan \\\"Riverside South, Manhattan\\\").[[60]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-64) Trump's last major construction project was the 92-story mixed-use [Trump International Hotel and Tower](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_International_Hotel_and_Tower_\\\\(Chicago\\\\) \\\"Trump International Hotel and Tower \\\\(Chicago\\\\)\\\") in Chicago, which opened in 2008. In 2024, [_The New York Times_ and ProPublica reported](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_International_Hotel_and_Tower_\\\\(Chicago\\\\)#Tax_deductions \\\"Trump International Hotel and Tower \\\\(Chicago\\\\)\\\") that the Internal Revenue Service was investigating whether he had twice written off losses incurred through construction cost overruns and lagging sales of residential units in the building he had declared to be worthless on his 2008 tax return.[[61]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-65)\\n#### Atlantic City casinos\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Trump_Taj_Mahal,_2007.jpg)Entrance of the [Trump Taj Mahal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Taj_Mahal \\\"Trump Taj Mahal\\\") in [Atlantic City](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_City \\\"Atlantic City\\\")\\nIn 1984, Trump opened [Harrah's at Trump Plaza](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harrah%27s_at_Trump_Plaza \\\"Harrah's at Trump Plaza\\\"), a hotel and casino, with financing and management help from the [Holiday Corporation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holiday_Corporation \\\"Holiday Corporation\\\").[[62]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-fall-66) It was unprofitable, and he paid Holiday $70 million in May 1986 to take sole control.[[63]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017\\\\[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA128_128\\\\]-67) In 1985, he bought the unopened Atlantic City Hilton Hotel and renamed it [Trump Castle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Castle \\\"Trump Castle\\\").[[64]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-68) Both casinos filed for [Chapter 11 bankruptcy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapter_11_bankruptcy \\\"Chapter 11 bankruptcy\\\") protection in 1992.[[65]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-69) Trump bought a third Atlantic City venue in 1988, the [Trump Taj Mahal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Taj_Mahal \\\"Trump Taj Mahal\\\"). It was financed with $675 million in [junk bonds](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Junk_bonds \\\"Junk bonds\\\") and completed for $1.1 billion, opening in April 1990.[[62]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-fall-66) He filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection in 1991. Under the provisions of the restructuring agreement, he gave up half his initial stake and personally guaranteed future performance.[[66]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-70) To reduce his $900 million of personal debt, he sold the [Trump Shuttle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Shuttle \\\"Trump Shuttle\\\") airline; his megayacht, the _[Trump Princess](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Princess \\\"Trump Princess\\\")_ , which had been leased to his casinos and kept docked; and other businesses.[[67]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-71) In 1995, Trump founded Trump Hotels & Casino Resorts (THCR), which assumed ownership of the Trump Plaza.[[68]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-72) THCR purchased the Taj Mahal and the Trump Castle in 1996 and went bankrupt in 2004 and 2009, leaving him with 10 percent ownership.[[62]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-fall-66) He remained chairman until 2009.[[69]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-73)\\n#### Golf clubs\\nIn 1985, Trump acquired the [Mar-a-Lago](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mar-a-Lago \\\"Mar-a-Lago\\\") estate in Palm Beach, Florida.[[70]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-74) In 1995, he converted the estate into a private club with an initiation fee and annual dues. He continued to use a wing of the house as a private residence.[[71]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-75) He declared the club his primary residence in 2019.[[54]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-moved-58) He began [building and buying golf courses](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_and_golf \\\"Donald Trump and golf\\\") in 1999, owning 17 golf courses by 2016.[[72]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-76)\\n### Licensing the Trump name\\nSee also: [List of things named after Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_things_named_after_Donald_Trump \\\"List of things named after Donald Trump\\\")\\nThe Trump Organization often licensed the Trump name for consumer products and services, including foodstuffs, apparel, learning courses, and home furnishings.[[73]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-neckties-77) Over 50 licensing or management deals involved Trump's name, generating at least $59 million for his companies.[[74]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-78) By 2018, only two consumer goods companies continued to license his name.[[73]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-neckties-77) During the 2000s, Trump licensed his name to residential property developments worldwide, 40 of which were never built.[[75]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024410-79)\\n### Side ventures\\nSee also: [Donald Trump and American football](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_and_American_football \\\"Donald Trump and American football\\\")\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_and_Doug_Flutie_at_a_press_conference_in_the_Trump_Tower.jpg)1985 New Jersey Generals press conference in [Trump Tower](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Tower \\\"Trump Tower\\\")\\nIn 1970, Trump invested $70,000 of his father's wealth to receive billing as coproducer of a Broadway comedy—and lost the money.[[76]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig2024109-80) After making low-ball bids for the [New York Mets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Mets \\\"New York Mets\\\") and the [Cleveland Indians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleveland_Indians \\\"Cleveland Indians\\\") baseball teams, in 1983 for about $6 million, he purchased the [New Jersey Generals](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Jersey_Generals \\\"New Jersey Generals\\\"), a team in the [United States Football League](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Football_League \\\"United States Football League\\\").[[77]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017175-81) The league folded after the 1985 season, largely due to his attempt to move to a fall schedule (when it would have competed with the [National Football League](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Football_League \\\"National Football League\\\") for audience) and his attempt to force a merger with the NFL by bringing an antitrust suit.[[78]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-82) Trump and his Plaza Hotel hosted several boxing matches at the [Atlantic City Convention Hall](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_City_Convention_Hall \\\"Atlantic City Convention Hall\\\").[[62]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-fall-66)[[79]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'DonnellRutherford1991137%E2%80%93143-83) In 1989 and 1990, he lent his name to the [Tour de Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tour_de_Trump \\\"Tour de Trump\\\") cycling stage race, an attempt to create an American equivalent of European races such as the [Tour de France](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tour_de_France \\\"Tour de France\\\") or the [Giro d'Italia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giro_d%27Italia \\\"Giro d'Italia\\\").[[80]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-84)\\nFrom 1986 to 1988, he purchased significant blocks of shares in various public companies while suggesting that he intended to take over the company and then sold his shares for a profit,[[81]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Buettner-190508-85) leading some observers to think he was engaged in [greenmail](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenmail \\\"Greenmail\\\").[[82]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-86) _The New York Times_ found that he initially made millions of dollars in such stock transactions, but \\\"lost most, if not all, of those gains after investors stopped taking his takeover talk seriously\\\".[[81]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Buettner-190508-85)\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_star_Hollywood_Walk_of_Fame.JPG)Trump's star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame\\nIn 1988, Trump purchased the [Eastern Air Lines Shuttle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Air_Lines_Shuttle \\\"Eastern Air Lines Shuttle\\\"), financing the purchase with $380 million (equivalent to $1.01 billion in 2024[[36]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-inflation-US-40)) in loans from a syndicate of 22 banks. He renamed the airline [Trump Shuttle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Shuttle \\\"Trump Shuttle\\\") and operated it until 1992.[[83]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-TA-87) He defaulted on his loans in 1991, and ownership passed to the banks.[[84]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-88) In 1996, he purchased the [Miss Universe](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miss_Universe \\\"Miss Universe\\\") pageants, including [Miss USA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miss_USA \\\"Miss USA\\\") and [Miss Teen USA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miss_Teen_USA \\\"Miss Teen USA\\\").[[85]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHaberman2022129%E2%80%93130-89) Due to disagreements with CBS about scheduling, he took both pageants to NBC in 2002.[[86]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-90)[[87]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-91) In 2007, he received a star on the [Hollywood Walk of Fame](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hollywood_Walk_of_Fame \\\"Hollywood Walk of Fame\\\") for his work as producer of Miss Universe.[[88]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-92) NBC and Univision dropped the pageants in June 2015 in reaction to his comments about Mexican immigrants.[[89]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-93)\\nIn 2005, Trump cofounded [Trump University](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_University \\\"Trump University\\\"), a company that sold real estate seminars for up to $35,000. After New York State authorities notified the company that its use of \\\"university\\\" violated state law (as it was not an academic institution), its name was changed to the Trump Entrepreneur Initiative in 2010.[[90]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTED'Antonio2015281%E2%80%93282-94) In 2013, the State of New York filed a $40 million civil suit against Trump University, alleging that the company made false statements and defrauded consumers. Additionally, two class actions were filed in federal court against Trump and his companies. Internal documents revealed that employees were instructed to use a hard-sell approach, and former employees testified that Trump University had defrauded or lied to its students.[[91]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTED'Antonio2015282%E2%80%93283-95) Shortly after he won the 2016 presidential election, he agreed to pay a total of $25 million to settle the three cases.[[92]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-96)\\n### Foundation\\nMain article: [Donald J. Trump Foundation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_J._Trump_Foundation \\\"Donald J. Trump Foundation\\\")\\nThe Donald J. Trump Foundation was a [private foundation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Private_foundation_\\\\(United_States\\\\) \\\"Private foundation \\\\(United States\\\\)\\\") established in 1988.[[93]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-97) From 1987 to 2006, Trump gave his foundation $5.4 million, which had been spent by the end of 2006. After donating a total of $65,000 in 2007–2008, he stopped donating any personal funds to the charity,[[94]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-retool-98) which received millions from other donors, including $5 million from [Vince McMahon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vince_McMahon \\\"Vince McMahon\\\").[[95]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-99) The foundation gave to health- and sports-related charities, conservative groups,[[96]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-100) and charities that held events at Trump properties.[[94]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-retool-98) In 2016, _[The Washington Post](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Washington_Post \\\"The Washington Post\\\")_ reported that the charity had committed several potential legal and ethical violations, including self-dealing and [tax evasion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_evasion \\\"Tax evasion\\\").[[97]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-101) Also in 2016, the New York attorney general stated the foundation had violated state law by soliciting donations without submitting to required annual external audits and ordered it to cease its fundraising activities in New York immediately.[[98]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-102) Trump's team announced in December 2016 that the foundation would be dissolved.[[99]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-103) In June 2018, the New York attorney general's office filed a civil suit against the foundation, Trump, and his adult children, seeking $2.8 million in restitution and additional penalties.[[100]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-104) In December 2018, the foundation ceased operation and disbursed its assets to other charities.[[101]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-105) In November 2019, a New York state judge ordered Trump to pay $2 million to a group of charities for misusing the foundation's funds, in part to finance his presidential campaign.[[102]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-106)\\n### Legal affairs and bankruptcies\\nMain article: [Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_and_business_legal_affairs_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump\\\")\\nAccording to a review of state and federal court files conducted by _[USA Today](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USA_Today \\\"USA Today\\\")_ in 2018, Trump and his businesses had been involved in more than 4,000 state and federal legal actions.[[103]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-107) While he has not filed for [personal bankruptcy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_bankruptcy \\\"Personal bankruptcy\\\"), his over-leveraged hotel and casino businesses in Atlantic City and New York filed for [Chapter 11 bankruptcy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapter_11_bankruptcy \\\"Chapter 11 bankruptcy\\\") protection six times between 1991 and 2009.[[46]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-TW-50) They continued to operate while the banks restructured debt and reduced his shares in the properties.[[46]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-TW-50) During the 1980s, more than 70 banks had lent Trump $4 billion.[[104]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-108) After his corporate bankruptcies of the early 1990s, most major banks, with the exception of [Deutsche Bank](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deutsche_Bank \\\"Deutsche Bank\\\"), declined to lend to him.[[105]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-109) After the [January 6 Capitol attack](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_6_Capitol_attack \\\"January 6 Capitol attack\\\"), the bank decided not to do business with him or his affiliated company in the future.[[106]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-110)\\n### Wealth\\nMain article: [Wealth of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wealth_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Wealth of Donald Trump\\\")\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Ivana_Trump_shakes_hands_with_Fahd_of_Saudi_Arabia.jpg)Trump (rightmost) and wife Ivana at a 1985 state dinner for King [Fahd of Saudi Arabia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fahd_of_Saudi_Arabia \\\"Fahd of Saudi Arabia\\\") with President [Ronald Reagan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronald_Reagan \\\"Ronald Reagan\\\") and First Lady [Nancy Reagan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nancy_Reagan \\\"Nancy Reagan\\\")\\nTrump has said he began his career with \\\"a small loan of a million dollars\\\" from his father and that he had to pay it back with interest.[[107]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-111) He borrowed at least $60 million from his father, largely did not repay the loans, and received another $413 million (2018 equivalent, adjusted for inflation) from his father's company.[[108]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-112)[[47]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Tax_Schemes-51) Posing as a Trump Organization official named \\\"[John Barron](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Barron_\\\\(pseudonym\\\\) \\\"John Barron \\\\(pseudonym\\\\)\\\")\\\", Trump called journalist Jonathan Greenberg in 1984, trying to get a higher ranking on the [_Forbes_ 400](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forbes_400 \\\"Forbes 400\\\") list of wealthy Americans.[[109]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-113) Trump self-reported his net worth over a wide range: from minus $900 million in 1990[[110]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-114) to $10 billion in 2015.[[111]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnston202120-115) In 2015, _[Forbes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forbes \\\"Forbes\\\")_ estimated his net worth at $4.5 billion, based on interviews with more than 80 sources.[[112]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-116) In 2025, the magazine estimated his net worth at $5.1 billion and ranked him the 700th wealthiest person in the world.[[113]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-117)\\n## Media career\\nMain article: [Media career of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Media_career_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Media career of Donald Trump\\\")\\nSee also: [Bibliography of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bibliography_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Bibliography of Donald Trump\\\")\\nTrump has published 19 books under his name, most written or cowritten by [ghostwriters](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghostwriters \\\"Ghostwriters\\\").[[114]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-118) His first book, _[The Art of the Deal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Art_of_the_Deal \\\"The Art of the Deal\\\")_ (1987), was a [_New York Times_ Best Seller](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times_Best_Seller_list \\\"The New York Times Best Seller list\\\"), and was credited by _[The New Yorker](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_Yorker \\\"The New Yorker\\\")_ with making Trump famous as an \\\"emblem of the successful tycoon\\\".[[115]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-JM-119) The book was ghostwritten by [Tony Schwartz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tony_Schwartz_\\\\(author\\\\) \\\"Tony Schwartz \\\\(author\\\\)\\\"), who is credited as a coauthor. Trump had cameos in many films and television shows from 1985 to 2001.[[116]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-120) Trump acquired his style of politics from [professional wrestling](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Professional_wrestling \\\"Professional wrestling\\\")—with its [staged fights](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staged_fight \\\"Staged fight\\\") and [name-calling](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Name-calling \\\"Name-calling\\\").[[117]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKlein201751%E2%80%9352Excerpt_reprinted_in_''\\\\[httpsharpersorgarchive201709w-w-e-the-people_Harper's\\\\]''-121) He sporadically appeared for the professional wrestling company [WWE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWE \\\"WWE\\\") from the late 1980s including [Wrestlemania 23](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wrestlemania_23 \\\"Wrestlemania 23\\\") in 2007.[[118]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGaufmanGanesh202469%E2%80%9370-122)[[119]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'Brien2020-123) Starting in the 1990s, Trump appeared 24 times as a guest on the nationally syndicated _[Howard Stern Show](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Howard_Stern_Show \\\"Howard Stern Show\\\")_.[[120]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017\\\\[httpsbooksgooglecombooksidx2jUDQAAQBAJpgPA166_166\\\\]-124) He had his own short-form talk radio program, _[Trumped!](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trumped! \\\"Trumped!\\\")_ , from 2004 to 2008.[[121]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-125) From 2011 until 2015, he was a guest commentator on _[Fox& Friends](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fox_%26_Friends \\\"Fox & Friends\\\")_.[[122]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-126) In 2021, Trump, who had been a member since 1989, resigned from [SAG-AFTRA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SAG-AFTRA \\\"SAG-AFTRA\\\") to avoid a disciplinary hearing regarding the January 6 attack.[[123]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-127) Two days later, the union permanently barred him.[[124]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-128)\\n###  _The Apprentice_ and _The Celebrity Apprentice_\\nMain articles: [The Apprentice (American TV series)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Apprentice_\\\\(American_TV_series\\\\) \\\"The Apprentice \\\\(American TV series\\\\)\\\") and [The Celebrity Apprentice](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Celebrity_Apprentice \\\"The Celebrity Apprentice\\\")\\nProducer [Mark Burnett](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Burnett \\\"Mark Burnett\\\") made Trump a television star[[125]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig20247\\\"Mark_Burnett,_the_television_producer_who_made_Trump_a_star,_did_not_just_hand_him_a_fortune.\\\"-129) when he created _The Apprentice_ , which Trump hosted from 2004 to 2015 (including variant _The Celebrity Apprentice_). On the shows, he was a superrich chief executive who eliminated contestants with the [catchphrase](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catchphrase \\\"Catchphrase\\\") \\\"you're fired\\\". _The New York Times_ called his portrayal \\\"a highly flattering, highly fictionalized version\\\" of himself.[[126]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-show-130) The shows remade Trump's image for millions of viewers nationwide.[[126]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-show-130)[[127]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-131) With the related licensing agreements, they earned him more than $400 million.[[128]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-132)\\n## Early political aspirations\\nFurther information: [Political career of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_career_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Political career of Donald Trump\\\")\\nTrump registered as a [Republican](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republican_Party_\\\\(United_States\\\\) \\\"Republican Party \\\\(United States\\\\)\\\") in 1987; a member of the [Independence Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independence_Party_of_New_York \\\"Independence Party of New York\\\"), the New York state affiliate of the [Reform Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reform_Party_of_the_United_States_of_America \\\"Reform Party of the United States of America\\\"), in 1999; a [Democrat](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Party_\\\\(United_States\\\\) \\\"Democratic Party \\\\(United States\\\\)\\\") in 2001; a Republican in 2009; [unaffiliated](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independent_politician \\\"Independent politician\\\") in 2011; and a Republican in 2012.[[129]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-133)\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_speaking_at_CPAC_2011_by_Mark_Taylor.jpg)Speaking at [CPAC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservative_Political_Action_Conference \\\"Conservative Political Action Conference\\\"), February 2011\\nIn 1987, Trump placed full-page advertisements in major newspapers[[130]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-hint-134) expressing his views on foreign policy and how to eliminate the federal budget deficit.[[131]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-135) In 1988, he approached [Lee Atwater](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_Atwater \\\"Lee Atwater\\\"), asking to be put into consideration to be Republican nominee [George H. W. Bush](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_H._W._Bush \\\"George H. W. Bush\\\")'s running mate. Bush found the request \\\"strange and unbelievable\\\".[[132]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMeacham2016326-136)[[133]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-137) Trump [was a candidate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_2000_presidential_campaign \\\"Donald Trump 2000 presidential campaign\\\") in the [2000 Reform Party presidential primaries](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2000_Reform_Party_presidential_primaries \\\"2000 Reform Party presidential primaries\\\") for three months before he withdrew in February 2000.[[134]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-138)[[135]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-139)[[136]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-140) In 2011, Trump considered challenging President [Barack Obama](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barack_Obama \\\"Barack Obama\\\") in [the 2012 election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_United_States_presidential_election \\\"2012 United States presidential election\\\"). He spoke at the [Conservative Political Action Conference](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservative_Political_Action_Conference \\\"Conservative Political Action Conference\\\") in February and gave speeches in states with early primaries.[[137]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-McA-141)[[138]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-142) In May 2011, he announced that he would not run.[[137]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-McA-141)\\n## 2016 presidential election\\nMain article: [2016 United States presidential election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_United_States_presidential_election \\\"2016 United States presidential election\\\")\\nFurther information: [Donald Trump 2016 presidential campaign](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_2016_presidential_campaign \\\"Donald Trump 2016 presidential campaign\\\"), [2016 Republican Party presidential primaries](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Republican_Party_presidential_primaries \\\"2016 Republican Party presidential primaries\\\"), and [First presidential transition of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_presidential_transition_of_Donald_Trump \\\"First presidential transition of Donald Trump\\\")\\nTrump announced his candidacy for the 2016 election in June 2015.[[139]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-143)[[140]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-144) He campaigned as a rich, successful businessman and an outsider without political experience,[[141]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-145)[[142]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-146)[[143]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-147) and claimed [media bias](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Media_bias \\\"Media bias\\\") against him.[[144]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-148)[[145]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-149) His campaign statements were often opaque and suggestive,[[146]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-150) and a record number were false.[[147]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-whoppers-151)[[148]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-152)[[149]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-153) He became the Republican front-runner in March 2016[[150]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-154) and was declared the presumptive Republican nominee in May.[[151]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-155)\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_by_Gage_Skidmore_5.jpg)Campaigning in Arizona, March 2016\\nTrump described NATO as \\\"obsolete\\\"[[152]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-156)[[153]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEEdwards2018\\\"On_the_campaign_trail,_Trump_repeatedly_called_North_Atlantic_Treaty_Organization_\\\\(NATO\\\\)_'obsolete'\\\"-157) and espoused views described by _The Washington Post_ as [noninterventionist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noninterventionist \\\"Noninterventionist\\\") and [protectionist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protectionist \\\"Protectionist\\\").[[154]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-158) His campaign platform emphasized renegotiating [U.S.–China relations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S.%E2%80%93China_relations \\\"U.S.–China relations\\\") and free trade agreements such as [NAFTA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NAFTA \\\"NAFTA\\\") and strongly enforcing immigration laws. Other campaign positions included pursuing [energy independence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_independence \\\"Energy independence\\\") while opposing climate change regulations, modernizing [services for veterans](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Veterans_Affairs#Veterans_Benefits_Administration \\\"United States Department of Veterans Affairs\\\"), repealing and replacing the [Affordable Care Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affordable_Care_Act \\\"Affordable Care Act\\\"), abolishing [Common Core](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Core \\\"Common Core\\\") education standards, [investing in infrastructure](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infrastructure-based_development \\\"Infrastructure-based development\\\"), simplifying the [tax code](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internal_Revenue_Code \\\"Internal Revenue Code\\\") while reducing taxes, and imposing [tariffs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariff \\\"Tariff\\\") on imports by companies that offshore jobs. He advocated increasing military spending and extreme vetting or banning of immigrants from Muslim-majority countries.[[155]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-159) He promised to build a [wall on the Mexico–U.S. border](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico%E2%80%93United_States_border_wall \\\"Mexico–United States border wall\\\") and vowed that Mexico would pay for it.[[156]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-160) He pledged to deport millions of [illegal immigrants residing in the U.S.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illegal_immigrant_population_of_the_United_States \\\"Illegal immigrant population of the United States\\\"),[[157]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-161) and criticized [birthright citizenship](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birthright_citizenship_in_the_United_States \\\"Birthright citizenship in the United States\\\") for incentivizing \\\"[anchor babies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anchor_babies \\\"Anchor babies\\\")\\\".[[158]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-162) According to an analysis in _[Political Science Quarterly](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_Science_Quarterly \\\"Political Science Quarterly\\\")_ , Trump made \\\"explicitly racist and sexist appeals to win over white voters\\\" during his 2016 presidential campaign.[[159]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTESchaffnerMacwilliamsNteta201831-163) In particular, his campaign launch speech drew criticism for claiming Mexican immigrants were \\\"bringing drugs, they're bringing crime, they're rapists\\\";[[160]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-164) in response, NBC fired him from _Celebrity Apprentice_.[[161]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-165)\\nTrump's FEC-required reports listed assets above $1.4 billion and outstanding debts of at least $315 million.[[162]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-disclosure-166)[[163]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-167) He did not release [his tax returns](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump%27s_tax_returns \\\"Donald Trump's tax returns\\\"), contrary to the practice of every major candidate since 1976 and his promises in 2014 and 2015 to do so if he ran for office.[[164]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-168)[[165]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-169) He said his tax returns were being [audited](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Income_tax_audit \\\"Income tax audit\\\"), and that his lawyers had advised him against releasing them.[[166]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-170) After a lengthy court battle to block release of his tax returns and other records to the [Manhattan district attorney](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan_district_attorney \\\"Manhattan district attorney\\\") for a criminal investigation, including two appeals by Trump to the [U.S. Supreme Court](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Supreme_Court \\\"U.S. Supreme Court\\\"), in February 2021 the high court allowed the records to be released to the prosecutor for review by a grand jury.[[167]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-171)[[168]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-172) In October 2016, portions of Trump's state filings for 1995 were leaked to a reporter from _The New York Times_. They show that he had declared a loss of $916 million that year, which could have let him avoid taxes for up to 18 years.[[169]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-173)\\nTrump won 306 pledged [electoral votes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electoral_College_\\\\(United_States\\\\) \\\"Electoral College \\\\(United States\\\\)\\\") versus 232 for Democratic candidate [Hillary Clinton](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hillary_Clinton \\\"Hillary Clinton\\\"). After elector [defections on both sides](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faithless_electors_in_the_2016_United_States_presidential_election \\\"Faithless electors in the 2016 United States presidential election\\\"), the official count was 304 to 227.[[170]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-174) The fifth person to be elected president [despite losing the popular vote](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_United_States_presidential_elections_in_which_the_winner_lost_the_popular_vote \\\"List of United States presidential elections in which the winner lost the popular vote\\\"),[[e]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-electoral-college-175) he received nearly 2.9 million fewer votes than Clinton, 46.3% to her 48.25%.[[171]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-176) He was the only president who neither served in the military nor held any government office prior to becoming president.[[172]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-177) His election marked the return of a Republican [undivided government](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divided_government_in_the_United_States \\\"Divided government in the United States\\\").[[f]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-178)[[173]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-179) Trump's victory [sparked protests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protests_against_Donald_Trump#Before_first_presidency \\\"Protests against Donald Trump\\\") in major U.S. cities.[[174]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-180)[[175]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-181)\\nMain article: [First presidency of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_presidency_of_Donald_Trump \\\"First presidency of Donald Trump\\\")\\nFor a chronological guide, see [Timeline of the Donald Trump presidencies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_Donald_Trump_presidencies \\\"Timeline of the Donald Trump presidencies\\\").\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_swearing_in_ceremony.jpg)Taking the [oath of office](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oath_of_office_of_the_President_of_the_United_States \\\"Oath of office of the President of the United States\\\"), administered by [Chief Justice](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_Justice_of_the_United_States \\\"Chief Justice of the United States\\\") [John G. Roberts Jr.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_G._Roberts_Jr. \\\"John G. Roberts Jr.\\\"), on January 20, 2017 [](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_official_portrait.jpg)Official portrait, 2017\\n### Early actions\\nSee also: [First 100 days of the first Trump presidency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_100_days_of_the_first_Trump_presidency \\\"First 100 days of the first Trump presidency\\\")\\n[Trump was inaugurated](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_inauguration_of_Donald_Trump \\\"First inauguration of Donald Trump\\\") on January 20, 2017. The day after his inauguration, an estimated 2.6 million people worldwide, including 500,000 in Washington, D.C., protested against him in the [Women's Marches](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_Women%27s_March \\\"2017 Women's March\\\").[[176]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-182) During his first week in office, Trump signed [six executive orders](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_executive_orders_in_the_first_Trump_presidency \\\"List of executive orders in the first Trump presidency\\\"), including authorizing procedures for repealing the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (\\\"Obamacare\\\"), withdrawal from the Trans-Pacific Partnership negotiations, advancement of the [Keystone XL](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keystone_Pipeline \\\"Keystone Pipeline\\\") and [Dakota Access Pipeline](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dakota_Access_Pipeline \\\"Dakota Access Pipeline\\\") projects, and planning for a wall along the U.S. border with Mexico.[[177]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-183)\\n### Conflicts of interest\\nSee also: [First presidency of Donald Trump § Ethics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_presidency_of_Donald_Trump#Ethics \\\"First presidency of Donald Trump\\\")\\nBefore being inaugurated, Trump moved his businesses into a [revocable trust](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revocable_trust \\\"Revocable trust\\\"),[[178]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-184)[[179]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-BBC041817-185) rather than a [blind trust](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blind_trust \\\"Blind trust\\\") or equivalent arrangement \\\"to cleanly sever himself from his business interests\\\".[[180]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-186) He continued to profit from his businesses and knew how his administration's policies affected them.[[179]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-BBC041817-185)[[181]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Venook-187) Although he said he would eschew \\\"new foreign deals\\\", the Trump Organization pursued operational expansions in Scotland, Dubai, and the Dominican Republic.[[179]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-BBC041817-185)[[181]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Venook-187) Lobbyists, foreign government officials, and Trump donors and allies generated hundreds of millions of dollars for his resorts and hotels.[[182]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-188) Trump was sued for violating the [Domestic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domestic_Emoluments_Clause \\\"Domestic Emoluments Clause\\\") and [Foreign Emoluments Clauses](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_Emoluments_Clause \\\"Foreign Emoluments Clause\\\") of the [U.S. Constitution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Constitution \\\"U.S. Constitution\\\"), the first time that the clauses had been substantively litigated.[[183]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-CRSRpt-189) One case was dismissed in lower court.[[184]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-190) Two were dismissed by the Supreme Court as moot after his term.[[185]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-191)\\nDuring the campaign, Trump had pledged to donate his presidential salary[[186]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-192) and profits from foreign patronage[[187]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-193) to the U.S. government. He donated his salary to federal agencies and publicized each donation until July 2020. Federal agencies surveyed by _The Washington Post_ in July 2021 reported not having received any gifts after that month.[[188]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-DAF-194) [Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Citizens_for_Responsibility_and_Ethics_in_Washington \\\"Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington\\\") reported in 2024 that he had donated $448,000 of an estimated $13.6 million in payments from foreign governments in his first term.[[189]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-195)\\n### Domestic policy\\nMain articles: [Domestic policy of the first Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domestic_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration \\\"Domestic policy of the first Trump administration\\\"), [Economic policy of the first Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration \\\"Economic policy of the first Trump administration\\\"), [Environmental policy of the first Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration \\\"Environmental policy of the first Trump administration\\\"), and [Social policy of the first Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration \\\"Social policy of the first Trump administration\\\")\\nTrump took office at the height of the longest [economic expansion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_expansion \\\"Economic expansion\\\") in American history,[[190]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-VanDam-196) which began in 2009 and continued until February 2020, when the [COVID-19 recession](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_recession \\\"COVID-19 recession\\\") began.[[191]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-197) In December 2017, he signed the [Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_Cuts_and_Jobs_Act_of_2017 \\\"Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017\\\"). It reduced tax rates for businesses and individuals and eliminated the penalty associated with the [Affordable Care Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affordable_Care_Act \\\"Affordable Care Act\\\")'s individual mandate.[[192]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-198)[[193]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-199) The Trump administration claimed that the act would not decrease government revenue, but 2018 revenues were 7.6 percent lower than projected.[[194]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-200) Under Trump, the federal budget deficit increased by almost 50 percent, to nearly $1 trillion in 2019.[[195]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-201) By the end of his term, the [U.S. national debt](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._national_debt \\\"U.S. national debt\\\") increased by 39 percent, reaching $27.75 trillion, and the U.S. [debt-to-GDP ratio](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debt-to-GDP_ratio \\\"Debt-to-GDP ratio\\\") hit a post-World War II high.[[196]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-202) Trump also failed to deliver on his campaign promise of a $1 trillion infrastructure spending plan.[[197]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-203)\\nTrump is the only modern U.S. president to leave office with a smaller workforce than when he took office, by three million people.[[190]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-VanDam-196)[[198]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-204) He rejects the [scientific consensus on climate change](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientific_consensus_on_climate_change \\\"Scientific consensus on climate change\\\").[[199]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-205)[[200]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-206)[[201]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-207)[[202]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-208) He reduced the budget for renewable energy research by 40 percent and reversed Obama-era policies directed at curbing climate change.[[203]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-209) He [withdrew from the Paris Agreement](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_and_the_Paris_Agreement \\\"United States and the Paris Agreement\\\"), making the U.S. the only nation to not ratify it.[[204]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-210) He aimed to boost the production and exports of [fossil fuels](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fossil_fuel \\\"Fossil fuel\\\").[[205]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-211)[[206]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-212) Natural gas expanded under Trump, but coal continued to decline.[[207]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-213)[[208]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Subramaniam-214) He rolled back more than 100 federal environmental regulations, including those that curbed [greenhouse gas emissions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenhouse_gas_emissions \\\"Greenhouse gas emissions\\\"), air and water pollution, and the use of toxic substances. He weakened protections for animals and environmental standards for federal infrastructure projects, and expanded permitted areas for drilling and resource extraction, such as allowing [drilling in the Arctic Refuge](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctic_Refuge_drilling_controversy \\\"Arctic Refuge drilling controversy\\\").[[209]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-215)\\nTrump dismantled federal regulations on health,[[210]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-216)[[211]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-midnight-217) labor,[[211]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-midnight-217) the environment,[[212]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-218)[[211]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-midnight-217) and other areas, including a bill that revoked the Obama-era regulation restricting the sale of firearms to severely mentally ill people.[[213]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-219) During his first six weeks in office, he delayed, suspended, or reversed ninety federal regulations,[[214]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-220) often \\\"after requests by the regulated industries\\\".[[215]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-221) The [Institute for Policy Integrity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institute_for_Policy_Integrity \\\"Institute for Policy Integrity\\\") found that 78 percent of his proposals were blocked by courts or did not prevail over litigation.[[216]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-222) During his campaign, Trump vowed to repeal and replace the [Affordable Care Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affordable_Care_Act \\\"Affordable Care Act\\\").[[217]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-223) In office, he scaled back the Act's implementation through executive orders.[[218]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-224)[[219]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-225) He expressed a desire to \\\"let Obamacare fail\\\"; his administration halved the [enrollment period](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annual_enrollment \\\"Annual enrollment\\\") and drastically reduced funding for enrollment promotion.[[220]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-226)[[221]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-227) In June 2018, the Trump administration [joined 18 Republican-led states in arguing before the Supreme Court](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California_v._Texas \\\"California v. Texas\\\") that the elimination of the financial penalties associated with the individual mandate had rendered the Act unconstitutional.[[222]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-StolbergACA-228)[[223]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-229) Their pleading would have eliminated [health insurance coverage](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Health_insurance_coverage_in_the_United_States \\\"Health insurance coverage in the United States\\\") for up to 23 million Americans, but was unsuccessful.[[222]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-StolbergACA-228) During the 2016 campaign, Trump promised to protect funding for Medicare and other social safety-net programs. In January 2020, he expressed willingness to consider cuts to them.[[224]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-230)\\nIn response to the [opioid epidemic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opioid_epidemic_in_the_United_States \\\"Opioid epidemic in the United States\\\"), Trump signed legislation in 2018 to increase funding for drug treatments, but was widely criticized for failing to make a concrete strategy.[[225]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-231) He barred organizations that provide abortions or abortion referrals from receiving federal funds.[[226]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-232) He said he supported \\\"traditional marriage\\\", but considered the [nationwide legality](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Same-sex_marriage_in_the_United_States \\\"Same-sex marriage in the United States\\\") of [same-sex marriage](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Same-sex_marriage \\\"Same-sex marriage\\\") \\\"settled\\\".[[227]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-233) His administration rolled back key components of the Obama administration's workplace protections against [discrimination of LGBTQ people](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discrimination_against_LGBT_people \\\"Discrimination against LGBT people\\\").[[228]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-234) His attempted rollback of anti-discrimination protections for [transgender](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transgender \\\"Transgender\\\") patients in August 2020 was halted by a federal judge after a Supreme Court ruling extended employees' civil rights protections to [gender identity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gender_identity \\\"Gender identity\\\") and sexual orientation.[[229]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-235) Trump has said he is [opposed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gun_politics_in_the_United_States \\\"Gun politics in the United States\\\") to [gun control](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gun_control \\\"Gun control\\\"), although his views have shifted over time.[[230]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-236) His administration took an [anti-marijuana position](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannabis_policy_of_the_Donald_Trump_administration \\\"Cannabis policy of the Donald Trump administration\\\"), revoking [Obama-era policies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cole_Memorandum \\\"Cole Memorandum\\\") that provided protections for states that legalized marijuana.[[231]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-237) He is a long-time advocate of capital punishment,[[232]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-238)[[233]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-239) and his administration oversaw the [federal government execute](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_punishment_by_the_United_States_federal_government \\\"Capital punishment by the United States federal government\\\") 13 prisoners, more than in the previous 56 years combined, ending a 17-year moratorium.[[234]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-240) In 2016, he said he supported the use of interrogation torture methods \\\"a hell of a lot worse than [waterboarding](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waterboarding \\\"Waterboarding\\\").\\\"[[235]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-241)[[236]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-242)\\n### Race relations\\n[Unite the Right rally](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unite_the_Right_rally \\\"Unite the Right rally\\\") comments\\nTrump's comments on the 2017 [Unite the Right rally](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unite_the_Right_rally \\\"Unite the Right rally\\\"), condemning \\\"this egregious display of hatred, bigotry and violence on many sides\\\" and stating that there were \\\"very fine people on both sides\\\", were criticized as implying a [moral equivalence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moral_equivalence \\\"Moral equivalence\\\") between the [white supremacist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_supremacist \\\"White supremacist\\\") demonstrators and the counter-protesters.[[237]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-243) In a January 2018 discussion of immigration legislation, he reportedly referred to El Salvador, Haiti, Honduras, and African nations as \\\"shithole countries\\\".[[238]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-244) His remarks were condemned as racist.[[239]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-245)\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_Visits_St._John%27s_Episcopal_Church_\\\\(49964153176\\\\).jpg)With a group of officials and advisors walking from the White House to St. John's Church, following the [forced removal of protesters at Lafayette Square](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_photo_op_at_St._John%27s_Church#Clearing_Lafayette_Square_and_St._John's \\\"Donald Trump photo op at St. John's Church\\\")\\nIn July 2019, Trump tweeted that four Democratic congresswomen—all minorities, three of whom are native-born Americans—should \\\"[go back](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Go_back_where_you_came_from \\\"Go back where you came from\\\")\\\" to the countries they \\\"came from\\\".[[240]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-246) Two days later the House of Representatives voted 240–187, mostly along party lines, to condemn his \\\"racist comments\\\".[[241]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-247) [White nationalist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_nationalist \\\"White nationalist\\\") publications and social media praised his remarks, which continued over the following days.[[242]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-248) He continued to make similar remarks during his 2020 campaign.[[243]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-249) In June 2020, during the [George Floyd protests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Floyd_protests \\\"George Floyd protests\\\"), federal law-enforcement officials used tear gas and other crowd control tactics to remove a largely peaceful crowd of lawful protesters from [Lafayette Square](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lafayette_Square,_Washington,_D.C. \\\"Lafayette Square, Washington, D.C.\\\"), outside the [White House](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House \\\"White House\\\").[[244]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-wb-250)[[245]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-bumpline-251) Trump then [posed with a Bible for a photo-op](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_photo-op_at_St._John%27s_Church \\\"Donald Trump photo-op at St. John's Church\\\") at the nearby [St. John's Episcopal Church](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._John%27s_Episcopal_Church,_Lafayette_Square \\\"St. John's Episcopal Church, Lafayette Square\\\"),[[244]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-wb-250)[[246]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-252)[[247]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-253) with religious leaders condemning both the treatment of protesters and the photo opportunity itself.[[248]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-254) Many retired military leaders and defense officials condemned his proposal to use the U.S. military against anti-police-brutality protesters.[[249]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-255)\\n### Pardons and commutations\\nFurther information: [List of people granted executive clemency in the first Trump presidency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_people_granted_executive_clemency_in_the_first_Trump_presidency \\\"List of people granted executive clemency in the first Trump presidency\\\")\\nDuring his first term, Trump granted 237 requests for clemency, fewer than all presidents since 1900 with the exception of [George H. W. Bush](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_H._W._Bush \\\"George H. W. Bush\\\") and [George W. Bush](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_W._Bush \\\"George W. Bush\\\").[[250]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-256) Only 25 of them had been vetted by the Justice Department's [Office of the Pardon Attorney](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_of_the_Pardon_Attorney \\\"Office of the Pardon Attorney\\\"); the others were granted to people with personal or political connections to him, his family, and his allies, or recommended by celebrities.[[251]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-road-257)[[252]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-OloDaw-258) In his last full day in office, he granted 73 pardons and commuted 70 sentences.[[253]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-259) Several Trump allies were not eligible for pardons under Justice Department rules, and in other cases the department had opposed clemency.[[251]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-road-257) The pardons of three military service members convicted of or charged with violent crimes were opposed by military leaders.[[254]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-260)\\n### Immigration\\nMain articles: [Immigration policy of the first Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immigration_policy_of_the_first_Trump_administration \\\"Immigration policy of the first Trump administration\\\") and [Mexico–United States border crisis § First Trump administration (2017–2021)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico%E2%80%93United_States_border_crisis#First_Trump_administration_\\\\(2017%E2%80%932021\\\\) \\\"Mexico–United States border crisis\\\")\\nFurther information: [Travel bans under the Trump administrations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Travel_bans_under_the_Trump_administrations \\\"Travel bans under the Trump administrations\\\"), [Trump administration family separation policy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_administration_family_separation_policy \\\"Trump administration family separation policy\\\"), and [Mexico–United States border wall § First Trump administration (2017–2021)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico%E2%80%93United_States_border_wall#First_Trump_administration_\\\\(2017%E2%80%932021\\\\) \\\"Mexico–United States border wall\\\")\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_visits_San_Diego_border_wall_prototypes.jpg)Examining border wall prototypes in [Otay Mesa, California](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otay_Mesa,_California \\\"Otay Mesa, California\\\")\\nAs president, Trump described illegal immigration as an \\\"invasion\\\" of the United States[[255]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-261) and drastically escalated immigration enforcement.[[256]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnson2017a-262)[[257]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnsonCuison-Villazor2019-263) He implemented harsh policies against asylum seekers[[257]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEJohnsonCuison-Villazor2019-263) and [deployed nearly 6,000 troops](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Faithful_Patriot \\\"Operation Faithful Patriot\\\") to the [U.S.–Mexico border](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S.%E2%80%93Mexico_border \\\"U.S.–Mexico border\\\") to stop illegal crossings.[[258]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-264) He reduced the number of [refugees admitted](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Refugee_Admissions_Program \\\"United States Refugee Admissions Program\\\") to record lows, from an annual limit of 110,000 before he took office to 15,000 in 2021.[[259]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-265)[[260]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-266)[[261]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-267) Trump also increased restrictions on granting [permanent residency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permanent_residency_in_the_United_States \\\"Permanent residency in the United States\\\") to [immigrants needing public benefits](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_charge_rule \\\"Public charge rule\\\").[[262]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-268) One of his central campaign promises was to build a [wall along the U.S.–Mexico border](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico%E2%80%93United_States_border_wall \\\"Mexico–United States border wall\\\");[[263]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-timm-269) during his first term, the U.S. built 73 miles (117 km) of wall in areas without barriers and 365 miles (587 km) to replace older barriers.[[264]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-270) In 2018, Trump's refusal to sign any [spending bill](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Appropriations_bill \\\"Appropriations bill\\\") unless it allocated funding for the border wall[[265]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-271) resulted in [the longest-ever federal government shutdown](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018%E2%80%932019_United_States_federal_government_shutdown \\\"2018–2019 United States federal government shutdown\\\"), for 35 days from December 2018 to January 2019.[[266]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Gambino-272)[[267]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-273) The shutdown ended after he agreed to fund the government without any funds for the wall.[[266]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Gambino-272) To avoid another shutdown, Congress passed a funding bill with $1.4 billion for border fencing in February.[[268]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Wilkie-274) Trump later declared a [national emergency on the southern border](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Emergency_Concerning_the_Southern_Border_of_the_United_States \\\"National Emergency Concerning the Southern Border of the United States\\\") to divert $6.1 billion of funding to the border wall[[268]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Wilkie-274) despite congressional disagreement.[[269]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-275)\\nIn January 2017, Trump signed [an executive order](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_13769 \\\"Executive Order 13769\\\") that [denied entry to citizens of six Muslim-majority countries](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_travel_ban \\\"Trump travel ban\\\") for four months and from Syria indefinitely.[[270]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-frontline-276)[[271]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-airport-277) The order caused [many protests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protests_against_Executive_Order_13769 \\\"Protests against Executive Order 13769\\\") and [legal challenges](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legal_challenges_to_the_Trump_travel_ban \\\"Legal challenges to the Trump travel ban\\\") that resulted in [nationwide injunctions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nationwide_injunction \\\"Nationwide injunction\\\").[[270]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-frontline-276)[[271]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-airport-277)[[272]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-278) [A revised order](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_13780 \\\"Executive Order 13780\\\") giving some exceptions was also blocked by courts,[[273]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-279)[[274]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-280) but [the Supreme Court ruled in June](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Refugee_Assistance_Project_v._Trump \\\"International Refugee Assistance Project v. Trump\\\") that the ban could be enforced on those lacking \\\"a _bona fide_ relationship with a person or entity\\\" in the U.S.[[275]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-281) Trump replaced the ban in September with [a presidential proclamation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidential_Proclamation_9645 \\\"Presidential Proclamation 9645\\\") extending travel bans to North Koreans, Chadians, and some Venezuelan officials, but excluded Iraq and Sudan.[[276]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-282) The Supreme Court allowed that version to go into effect in December 2017,[[277]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-283) and ultimately upheld the ban in 2019.[[278]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-284) From 2017 to 2018, the Trump administration had [a policy of family separation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_administration_family_separation_policy \\\"Trump administration family separation policy\\\") that separated over 4,400 children of illegal immigrants from their parents at the U.S.–Mexico border,[[279]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-285)[[280]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Spagat-286) an unprecedented[[281]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Domonoske-287) policy sparked public outrage in the country.[[282]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-288) Despite Trump initially blaming Democrats[[283]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-289)[[284]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-290) and insisting he could not stop the policy with an executive order, he acceded to public pressure in June 2018 and mandated that illegal immigrant families be detained together unless \\\"there is a concern\\\" of risk for the child.[[285]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-291)[[286]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-292) A judge later ordered that the families be reunited and further separations stopped except in limited circumstances,[[287]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-293)[[288]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-294) though over 1,000 additional children were separated from their families after the order.[[280]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Spagat-286)\\n### Foreign policy\\nMain articles: [Foreign policy of the first Donald Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_the_first_Donald_Trump_administration \\\"Foreign policy of the first Donald Trump administration\\\") and [Tariffs in the first Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariffs_in_the_first_Trump_administration \\\"Tariffs in the first Trump administration\\\")\\nFurther information: [Russia–United States relations § First Trump administration (2017–2021)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russia%E2%80%93United_States_relations#First_Trump_administration_\\\\(2017%E2%80%932021\\\\) \\\"Russia–United States relations\\\"), [China–United States relations § First Trump administration (2017–2021)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93United_States_relations#First_Trump_administration_\\\\(2017%E2%80%932021\\\\) \\\"China–United States relations\\\"), [2017–2018 North Korea crisis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017%E2%80%932018_North_Korea_crisis \\\"2017–2018 North Korea crisis\\\"), and [2018–19 Korean peace process](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018%E2%80%9319_Korean_peace_process \\\"2018–19 Korean peace process\\\")\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:-G7Biarritz_\\\\(48616362963\\\\).jpg)[G7](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Group_of_Seven \\\"Group of Seven\\\") leaders at the [45th summit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/45th_G7_summit \\\"45th G7 summit\\\") in France, 2019\\nTrump described himself as a \\\"nationalist\\\"[[289]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-295) and his foreign policy as \\\"[America First](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/America_First \\\"America First\\\")\\\".[[290]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Bennhold-296) He supported [populist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Populist \\\"Populist\\\"), [neo-nationalist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neo-nationalist \\\"Neo-nationalist\\\"), and authoritarian governments.[[291]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-297) Unpredictability, uncertainty, and inconsistency characterized foreign relations during his tenure.[[290]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Bennhold-296)[[292]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcGurk2020-298) Relations between the U.S. and its European allies were strained under Trump.[[293]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-299) He criticized [NATO allies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Member_states_of_NATO \\\"Member states of NATO\\\") and privately suggested that the U.S. should [withdraw from NATO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Withdrawal_from_NATO#United_States \\\"Withdrawal from NATO\\\").[[294]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-300)[[295]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-301) Trump supported many of the policies of Israeli prime minister [Benjamin Netanyahu](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjamin_Netanyahu \\\"Benjamin Netanyahu\\\").[[296]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-302) In 2020, Trump hosted the signing of the [Abraham Accords](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Accords \\\"Abraham Accords\\\") between Israel and the [United Arab Emirates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Arab_Emirates \\\"United Arab Emirates\\\") and [Bahrain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahrain \\\"Bahrain\\\") to normalize their foreign relations.[[297]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-303)\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_%26_the_First_Lady%27s_Trip_to_Europe_\\\\(42547210635\\\\).jpg)Shaking hands with Russian president Vladimir Putin during the [2018 summit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Russia%E2%80%93United_States_summit \\\"2018 Russia–United States summit\\\") in [Helsinki](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helsinki \\\"Helsinki\\\"), Finland\\nTrump began [a trade war with China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93United_States_trade_war \\\"China–United States trade war\\\") in 2018 after imposing tariffs and other trade barriers he said would force China to end longstanding unfair trade practice and [intellectual property infringement](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allegations_of_intellectual_property_infringement_by_China \\\"Allegations of intellectual property infringement by China\\\").[[298]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-304) Trump weakened the toughest U.S. sanctions imposed after the [2014 Russian annexation of Crimea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2014_Russian_annexation_of_Crimea \\\"2014 Russian annexation of Crimea\\\").[[299]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-305)[[300]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-306) Trump praised and, according to some critics, rarely criticized Russian president [Vladimir Putin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_Putin \\\"Vladimir Putin\\\"),[[301]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-307)[[302]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-308) though he opposed some actions of Russia's government.[[303]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-309) He withdrew the U.S. from the [Intermediate-Range Nuclear Forces Treaty](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intermediate-Range_Nuclear_Forces_Treaty \\\"Intermediate-Range Nuclear Forces Treaty\\\"), citing alleged Russian noncompliance,[[304]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-310) and supported Russia's possible return to the [G7](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G7 \\\"G7\\\").[[305]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-G8-311) As [North Korea's nuclear weapons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Korea%27s_nuclear_weapons \\\"North Korea's nuclear weapons\\\") were increasingly seen as a serious threat,[[306]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-312) Trump became the first sitting U.S. president to meet a North Korean leader, meeting [Kim Jong Un](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kim_Jong_Un \\\"Kim Jong Un\\\") three times: [in Singapore](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_North_Korea%E2%80%93United_States_Singapore_Summit \\\"2018 North Korea–United States Singapore Summit\\\") in June 2018, [in Hanoi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019_North_Korea%E2%80%93United_States_Hanoi_Summit \\\"2019 North Korea–United States Hanoi Summit\\\") in February 2019, and [in the Korean Demilitarized Zone](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019_Koreas%E2%80%93United_States_DMZ_Summit \\\"2019 Koreas–United States DMZ Summit\\\") in June 2019.[[307]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-313) Talks in October 2019 broke down and no [denuclearization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denuclearization \\\"Denuclearization\\\") agreement was reached.[[308]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-314)[[309]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-315)\\n### Personnel\\nMain articles: [Political appointments of the first Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_appointments_of_the_first_Trump_administration \\\"Political appointments of the first Trump administration\\\") and [First cabinet of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_cabinet_of_Donald_Trump \\\"First cabinet of Donald Trump\\\")\\nFurther information: [Hiring and personnel of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiring_and_personnel_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Hiring and personnel of Donald Trump\\\")\\nBy the end of Trump's first year in office, 34 percent of his original staff had resigned, been fired, or been reassigned.[[310]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-316) As of early July 2018[[update]](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Donald_Trump&action=edit), 61 percent of his senior aides had left[[311]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-317) and 141 staffers had left in the previous year.[[312]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-318) Both figures set a record for recent presidents.[[313]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Keith-319) Close personal aides to Trump quit or were forced out.[[314]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Brookings-320) He publicly disparaged several of his former top officials.[[315]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-321)\\nTrump had four [White House chiefs of staff](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_chiefs_of_staff \\\"White House chiefs of staff\\\"), marginalizing or pushing out several.[[316]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Keither-322) In May 2017, he [dismissed FBI director James Comey](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dismissal_of_James_Comey \\\"Dismissal of James Comey\\\"), saying a few days later that he was concerned about Comey's role in the Trump–Russia investigations.[[317]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-323)[[318]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-324) Three of Trump's 15 original cabinet members left or were forced to resign within his first year.[[319]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-538_Cabinet-325)[[314]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Brookings-320) Trump was slow to appoint second-tier officials in the executive branch, saying many of the positions are unnecessary. In October 2017, there were hundreds of sub-cabinet positions without a nominee.[[320]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-326) By January 8, 2019, of 706 key positions, 433 had been filled and he had no nominee for 264.[[321]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-327)\\n### Judiciary\\nFurther information: [List of federal judges appointed by Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_federal_judges_appointed_by_Donald_Trump \\\"List of federal judges appointed by Donald Trump\\\") and [Donald Trump judicial appointment controversies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_judicial_appointment_controversies \\\"Donald Trump judicial appointment controversies\\\")\\nTrump appointed 226 [federal judges](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_federal_judge \\\"United States federal judge\\\"), including 54 to the [courts of appeals](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_courts_of_appeals \\\"United States courts of appeals\\\") and [three](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_Supreme_Court_candidates \\\"Donald Trump Supreme Court candidates\\\") to the [Supreme Court](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Court_of_the_United_States \\\"Supreme Court of the United States\\\"): [Neil Gorsuch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neil_Gorsuch \\\"Neil Gorsuch\\\"), [Brett Kavanaugh](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brett_Kavanaugh \\\"Brett Kavanaugh\\\"), and [Amy Coney Barrett](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amy_Coney_Barrett \\\"Amy Coney Barrett\\\").[[322]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-328) His Supreme Court appointments politically shifted the Court to the right.[[323]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-329)[[324]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-330)[[325]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-331) In the 2016 campaign, he pledged that _[Roe v. Wade](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roe_v._Wade \\\"Roe v. Wade\\\")_ would be overturned \\\"automatically\\\" if he were elected and given the opportunity to appoint two or three anti-abortion justices. He later took credit when _Roe_ was overturned by _[Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dobbs_v._Jackson_Women%27s_Health_Organization \\\"Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization\\\")_ in 2022; all three of his Supreme Court nominees voted with the majority.[[326]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-332)[[327]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-333) Trump disparaged courts and judges he disagreed with, often in personal terms, and questioned the judiciary's constitutional authority. His attacks on courts drew rebukes from observers, including sitting federal judges, concerned about the effect of his statements on the [judicial independence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judicial_independence \\\"Judicial independence\\\") and public confidence in the judiciary.[[328]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-334)[[329]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-335)\\n### COVID-19 pandemic\\nMain article: [COVID-19 pandemic in the United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic_in_the_United_States \\\"COVID-19 pandemic in the United States\\\")\\nFurther information: [U.S. federal government response to the COVID-19 pandemic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._federal_government_response_to_the_COVID-19_pandemic \\\"U.S. federal government response to the COVID-19 pandemic\\\") and [Communication of the Trump administration during the COVID-19 pandemic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communication_of_the_Trump_administration_during_the_COVID-19_pandemic \\\"Communication of the Trump administration during the COVID-19 pandemic\\\")\\nSee also: [Economic impact of the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_impact_of_the_COVID-19_pandemic_in_the_United_States \\\"Economic impact of the COVID-19 pandemic in the United States\\\")\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:White_House_Press_Briefing_\\\\(49666120807\\\\).jpg)Conducting a COVID-19 press briefing with members of the [White House Coronavirus Task Force](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_Coronavirus_Task_Force \\\"White House Coronavirus Task Force\\\") on March 15, 2020\\nTrump initially ignored public health warnings and calls for action from health officials within his administration.[[330]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-336) Trump established the [White House Coronavirus Task Force](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_Coronavirus_Task_Force \\\"White House Coronavirus Task Force\\\") on January 29.[[331]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-337) On March 27, he signed into law the [CARES Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CARES_Act \\\"CARES Act\\\")—a $2.2 trillion bipartisan [economic stimulus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_stimulus \\\"Economic stimulus\\\") bill—the largest stimulus in U.S. history.[[332]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-NY_Times_stimulus_united_senate-338)[[333]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-AP_Trump_signs_2.2-339) After weeks of attacks to draw attention away from his slow response, Trump halted funding of the [World Health Organization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Health_Organization \\\"World Health Organization\\\") in April.[[334]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-340) In April 2020, Republican-connected groups organized [anti-lockdown protests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protests_in_the_United_States_over_responses_to_the_COVID-19_pandemic \\\"Protests in the United States over responses to the COVID-19 pandemic\\\") against the measures state governments were taking to combat the pandemic;[[335]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-341)[[336]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-342) Trump encouraged the protests on [Twitter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twitter \\\"Twitter\\\"),[[337]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-343) although the targeted states did not meet his administration's guidelines for reopening.[[338]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-344) He repeatedly pressured federal health agencies to take actions he favored,[[339]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-CNN-testing-pressure-345) such as approving unproven treatments.[[340]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-346)[[341]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-pressed-347) In October, Trump was hospitalized at [Walter Reed National Military Medical Center](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_Reed_National_Military_Medical_Center \\\"Walter Reed National Military Medical Center\\\") for three days with a severe case of [COVID-19](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19 \\\"COVID-19\\\").[[342]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-348)\\n### Investigations\\nFurther information: [Russian interference in the 2016 United States elections](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_interference_in_the_2016_United_States_elections \\\"Russian interference in the 2016 United States elections\\\"), [Mueller special counsel investigation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mueller_special_counsel_investigation \\\"Mueller special counsel investigation\\\"), and [Mueller report](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mueller_report \\\"Mueller report\\\")\\nAfter he assumed office, Trump was the subject of increasing [Justice Department](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Justice \\\"United States Department of Justice\\\") and congressional scrutiny, with investigations covering his election campaign, transition, and inauguration, actions taken during his presidency, his [private businesses](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Trump_Organization \\\"The Trump Organization\\\"), personal taxes, and [charitable foundation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_J._Trump_Foundation \\\"Donald J. Trump Foundation\\\").[[343]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-349) There were ten federal criminal investigations, eight state and local investigations, and twelve congressional investigations.[[344]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-350)\\nIn July 2016, the [FBI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FBI \\\"FBI\\\") launched [Crossfire Hurricane](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crossfire_Hurricane_\\\\(FBI_investigation\\\\) \\\"Crossfire Hurricane \\\\(FBI investigation\\\\)\\\"), an investigation into possible links between Russia and Trump's 2016 campaign.[[345]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-351) After Trump fired Comey in May 2017, the FBI opened a second investigation into Trump's personal and [business dealings with Russia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business_projects_of_Donald_Trump_in_Russia \\\"Business projects of Donald Trump in Russia\\\").[[346]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-352) In January 2017, three U.S. intelligence agencies jointly stated with \\\"high confidence\\\" that [Russia interfered in the 2016 presidential election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russian_interference_in_the_2016_United_States_elections \\\"Russian interference in the 2016 United States elections\\\") to favor Trump.[[347]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-353)[[348]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-354) Many suspicious[[349]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-355) [links between Trump associates and Russian officials](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Links_between_Trump_associates_and_Russian_officials \\\"Links between Trump associates and Russian officials\\\") were discovered.[[350]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-356)[[351]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-357)[[352]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-358) Trump told Russian officials he was unconcerned about Russia's election interference.[[353]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-359) Crossfire Hurricane was later transferred to [Robert Mueller](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Mueller \\\"Robert Mueller\\\")'s [special counsel investigation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mueller_special_counsel_investigation \\\"Mueller special counsel investigation\\\");[[354]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-360) the investigation into Trump's ties to Russia was ended by Deputy Attorney General [Rod Rosenstein](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rod_Rosenstein \\\"Rod Rosenstein\\\") after he told the FBI that Mueller would pursue the matter.[[355]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-never-361)[[356]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-362) At the request of Rosenstein, the Mueller investigation examined criminal matters \\\"in connection with Russia's 2016 election interference\\\".[[355]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-never-361) Mueller submitted [his final report](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mueller_report \\\"Mueller report\\\") in March 2019.[[357]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-363) The report found that Russia did interfere in 2016 to favor Trump[[358]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-364) and that Trump and his campaign welcomed and encouraged the effort,[[359]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-365)[[360]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-366)[[361]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-367) but that the evidence \\\"did not establish\\\" that Trump campaign members conspired or coordinated with Russia.[[362]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-368)[[363]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-takeaways-369) Trump claimed the report exonerated him despite Mueller writing that it did not.[[364]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-370) The report also detailed potential obstruction of justice by Trump but \\\"did not draw ultimate conclusions\\\"[[365]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-371)[[366]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-372) and left the decision to charge the laws to Congress.[[367]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-LM-373)\\nIn April 2019, the [House Oversight Committee](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_Oversight_Committee \\\"House Oversight Committee\\\") issued [subpoenas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subpoena \\\"Subpoena\\\") seeking financial details from Trump's banks, Deutsche Bank and [Capital One](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_One \\\"Capital One\\\"), and his accounting firm, [Mazars USA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mazars_USA \\\"Mazars USA\\\"). He sued the banks, Mazars, and committee chair [Elijah Cummings](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elijah_Cummings \\\"Elijah Cummings\\\") to prevent the disclosures.[[368]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-374) In May, two judges ruled that both Mazars and the banks must comply with the subpoenas;[[369]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-375)[[370]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-376)[[371]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-377) Trump's attorneys appealed.[[372]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-378) In September 2022, Trump and the committee agreed to a settlement regarding Mazars, and the firm began turning over documents.[[373]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-379)\\n### Impeachments\\nMain articles: [First impeachment of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump \\\"First impeachment of Donald Trump\\\") and [Second impeachment of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Second impeachment of Donald Trump\\\")\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump_Delivers_Remarks_\\\\(49498772251\\\\).jpg)Displaying the headline \\\"Trump acquitted\\\" in 2020\\nTrump was [impeached](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impeachment_in_the_United_States \\\"Impeachment in the United States\\\") twice by the House of Representatives, though acquitted by the Senate on both occasions. The [first impeachment](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump \\\"First impeachment of Donald Trump\\\") arose from a whistleblower complaint that in July 2019 Trump [had pressured](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019_Trump%E2%80%93Ukraine_scandal \\\"2019 Trump–Ukraine scandal\\\") Ukrainian president [Volodymyr Zelenskyy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volodymyr_Zelenskyy \\\"Volodymyr Zelenskyy\\\") to investigate Joe and Hunter Biden,[[374]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-undermine-380) in an attempt to gain an advantage in the [2020 presidential election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_presidential_election \\\"2020 United States presidential election\\\").[[375]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-381) In December 2019, the House voted to impeach Trump for [abuse of power](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abuse_of_power \\\"Abuse of power\\\") and [obstruction of Congress](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obstruction_of_Congress \\\"Obstruction of Congress\\\"),[[376]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-382) and the Senate acquitted him in February 2020.[[377]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-383)\\nThe [second impeachment](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_impeachment_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Second impeachment of Donald Trump\\\") came after the January 6 Capitol attack, for which the House charged Trump with [incitement of insurrection](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incitement_of_insurrection \\\"Incitement of insurrection\\\") on January 13, 2021.[[378]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-SecondImpeachment-384) Trump left office on January 20, and was acquitted on February 13. Seven Republican senators voted for conviction.[[379]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-385)\\n## 2020 presidential election\\nFurther information: [2020 United States presidential election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_presidential_election \\\"2020 United States presidential election\\\") and [Donald Trump 2020 presidential campaign](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_2020_presidential_campaign \\\"Donald Trump 2020 presidential campaign\\\")\\nTrump filed to run for reelection only a few hours after becoming president in 2017.[[380]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-386) He held his first reelection rally less than a month after taking office[[381]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-387) and officially became the [Republican nominee](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_Republican_Party_presidential_primaries \\\"2020 Republican Party presidential primaries\\\") in August 2020.[[382]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-388) Trump's campaign focused on crime, claiming that cities would descend into lawlessness if Democratic nominee Joe Biden won.[[383]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-389) He repeatedly misrepresented Biden's positions[[384]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-390)[[385]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-391) and appealed to racism.[[386]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-392) Starting in early 2020, Trump sowed doubts about the election, claiming without evidence that it would be rigged and that widespread use of mail balloting would produce massive election fraud.[[387]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-393)[[388]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-394) He [blocked funding for the U.S. Postal Service](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_Postal_Service_crisis \\\"2020 United States Postal Service crisis\\\"), saying he wanted to prevent any increase in voting by mail.[[389]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-395) He repeatedly refused to say whether he would accept the results if he lost and commit to a [peaceful transition of power](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peaceful_transition_of_power \\\"Peaceful transition of power\\\").[[390]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-396)[[391]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-397)\\n### Loss to Biden and rejection of outcome\\nFurther information: [Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempts_to_overturn_the_2020_United_States_presidential_election \\\"Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election\\\") and [2020–21 United States election protests](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020%E2%80%9321_United_States_election_protests \\\"2020–21 United States election protests\\\")\\nBiden won the November 2020 election, receiving 81.3 million votes (51.3 percent) to Trump's 74.2 million (46.8 percent)[[392]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-vote1-398)[[393]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-vote2-399) and 306 electoral votes to Trump's 232.[[394]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-formalize-400) The [Electoral College](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Electoral_College \\\"United States Electoral College\\\") formalized Biden's victory on December 14.[[394]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-formalize-400) Trump declared victory before the results were known on the morning after the election.[[395]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-401) Days later, when Biden was projected the winner, Trump baselessly alleged election fraud.[[396]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-402) As part of [an effort to overturn the results](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempts_to_overturn_the_2020_United_States_presidential_election \\\"Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election\\\"), Trump and his allies filed many [lawsuits challenging the results](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-election_lawsuits_related_to_the_2020_U.S._presidential_election \\\"Post-election lawsuits related to the 2020 U.S. presidential election\\\"), which were rejected by at least 86 judges in both state and federal courts for having no factual or legal basis.[[397]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-403)[[398]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-404)\\nTrump's allegations were also refuted by state election officials,[[399]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-405) and the Supreme Court declined to hear [a case asking it to overturn the results](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Texas_v._Pennsylvania \\\"Texas v. Pennsylvania\\\") in four states won by Biden.[[400]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-406) Trump repeatedly sought help to overturn the results, personally pressuring Republican local and state office-holders,[[401]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-amyG-407) Republican legislators,[[402]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-pressure-408) the Justice Department,[[403]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-409) and Vice President Pence,[[404]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-410) urging actions such as [replacing presidential electors](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_fake_electors_plot \\\"Trump fake electors plot\\\"),[[402]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-pressure-408) or that Georgia officials [\\\"find\\\" votes and announce](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump%E2%80%93Raffensperger_phone_call \\\"Trump–Raffensperger phone call\\\") a \\\"recalculated\\\" result.[[401]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-amyG-407) In the weeks after the election, Trump withdrew from public activities.[[405]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-411) He initially blocked government officials from cooperating in [Biden's presidential transition](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidential_transition_of_Joe_Biden \\\"Presidential transition of Joe Biden\\\").[[406]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-412)[[407]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-413) After three weeks, the administrator of the [General Services Administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Services_Administration \\\"General Services Administration\\\") declared Biden the \\\"apparent winner\\\" of the election, allowing the disbursement of transition resources to his team.[[408]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-414) While Trump said he recommended that the GSA begin transition protocols, he still did not formally concede.[[409]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-415)[[410]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-416) Trump did not attend [Biden's inauguration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biden%27s_inauguration \\\"Biden's inauguration\\\") on January 20.[[411]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-417)\\n### January 6 Capitol attack\\nMain article: [January 6 United States Capitol attack](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_6_United_States_Capitol_attack \\\"January 6 United States Capitol attack\\\")\\nIn December 2020, reports emerged that U.S. military leaders were on high alert, and ranking officers had discussed what to do if Trump declared [martial law](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martial_law \\\"Martial law\\\").[[412]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-418) CIA director [Gina Haspel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gina_Haspel \\\"Gina Haspel\\\") and Army general [Mark Milley](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Milley \\\"Mark Milley\\\"), chairman of the [Joint Chiefs of Staff](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joint_Chiefs_of_Staff \\\"Joint Chiefs of Staff\\\"), grew concerned that Trump might attempt a [coup](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coup \\\"Coup\\\") or military action against China or Iran.[[413]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-419)[[414]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-420) Milley insisted that he be consulted about any military orders from Trump, including the use of nuclear weapons.[[415]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-421)[[416]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-422)\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2021_storming_of_the_United_States_Capitol_DSC09254-2_\\\\(50820534063\\\\)_\\\\(retouched\\\\).jpg)A crowd of Trump supporters during the attack\\nAt noon on January 6, 2021, while [Congress was certifying the presidential election results](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_United_States_Electoral_College_vote_count \\\"2021 United States Electoral College vote count\\\") in the [U.S. Capitol](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._Capitol \\\"U.S. Capitol\\\"), Trump held [a rally at the nearby Ellipse](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_6_Trump_rally \\\"January 6 Trump rally\\\"). Speaking from behind a glass barrier,[[417]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-423) he called for the election to be overturned and urged his supporters to \\\"fight like hell\\\" and \\\"take back our country\\\" by marching to the Capitol.[[418]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-424) His supporters then [formed a mob that broke into the building](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/January_6_United_States_Capitol_attack \\\"January 6 United States Capitol attack\\\"), disrupting certification and causing the evacuation of Congress.[[419]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-425) During the attack, Trump posted on social media but did not ask the rioters to disperse. In a [tweet](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tweet_\\\\(social_media\\\\) \\\"Tweet \\\\(social media\\\\)\\\") at 6 p.m., he told them to \\\"go home with love & in peace\\\", called them \\\"great patriots\\\", and restated that he had won the election.[[420]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-426) Congress later reconvened and confirmed Biden's victory in the early hours of January 7.[[421]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-427)\\nMore than 140 police officers were injured, and five people died during or after the attack.[[422]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-428)[[423]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-429) The event has been described as an attempted [self-coup](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-coup \\\"Self-coup\\\") by Trump.[[g]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-433)\\nUpon leaving the White House, Trump began living at Mar-a-Lago, establishing an office there as provided for by the [Former Presidents Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Former_Presidents_Act \\\"Former Presidents Act\\\").[[427]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-434) His continuing [false claims concerning the 2020 election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_lie#Donald_Trump's_false_claims_of_a_stolen_election \\\"Big lie\\\") were commonly referred to as the \\\"[big lie](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_lie \\\"Big lie\\\")\\\" by his critics, although in May 2021, he and many of his supporters began using the term to refer to the election itself.[[428]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-435)[[429]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-key2-436) The Republican Party used his false claims about the election to justify [imposing new voting restrictions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republican_efforts_to_restrict_voting_following_the_2020_presidential_election \\\"Republican efforts to restrict voting following the 2020 presidential election\\\") in its favor.[[430]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-437)[[431]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-438)[[432]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-439) As of July 2022, he continued to pressure state legislators to overturn the election.[[433]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-440) Unlike other former presidents, Trump continued to dominate his party; a 2022 profile in _The New York Times_ described him as a modern [party boss](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Party_boss \\\"Party boss\\\").[[434]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-PB2-441) He continued fundraising, raising a [war chest](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_chest \\\"War chest\\\") containing more than twice that of the Republican Party, and profited from fundraisers many Republican candidates held at Mar-a-Lago. Much of his focus was on party governance and installing in key posts officials loyal to him.[[434]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-PB2-441) In the [2022 midterm elections](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_midterm_elections \\\"2022 midterm elections\\\"), he endorsed over 200 candidates for various offices.[[435]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-442) In February 2021, he registered a new company, [Trump Media & Technology Group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Media_%26_Technology_Group \\\"Trump Media & Technology Group\\\") (TMTG), for providing \\\"social networking services\\\" to U.S. customers.[[436]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-443)[[437]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-444) In March 2024, TMTG merged with [special-purpose acquisition company](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special-purpose_acquisition_company \\\"Special-purpose acquisition company\\\") [Digital World Acquisition](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_World_Acquisition_Corp. \\\"Digital World Acquisition Corp.\\\") and became a [public company](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_company \\\"Public company\\\").[[438]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-445) In February 2022, TMTG launched [Truth Social](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truth_Social \\\"Truth Social\\\"), a social media platform.[[439]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-446)\\n### Legal issues\\nSee also: [Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_and_business_legal_affairs_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump\\\") and [Legal affairs of the first Donald Trump presidency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legal_affairs_of_the_first_Donald_Trump_presidency \\\"Legal affairs of the first Donald Trump presidency\\\")\\nIn 2019, journalist [E. Jean Carroll](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E._Jean_Carroll \\\"E. Jean Carroll\\\") accused Trump of raping her in the 1990s and [sued him for defamation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E._Jean_Carroll_v._Donald_J._Trump \\\"E. Jean Carroll v. Donald J. Trump\\\") over his denial.[[440]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-447) Carroll sued him again in 2022 for [battery](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battery_\\\\(crime\\\\) \\\"Battery \\\\(crime\\\\)\\\") and more defamation.[[441]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-448) He was [found liable](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legal_liability \\\"Legal liability\\\") for sexual abuse and defamation and ordered to pay $5 million in one case[[442]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Sullivan2023-449) and $83.3 million in the other.[[442]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Sullivan2023-449)[[443]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-SA-450) In 2022, [New York filed a civil lawsuit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_business_fraud_lawsuit_against_the_Trump_Organization \\\"New York business fraud lawsuit against the Trump Organization\\\") against Trump accusing him of inflating the Trump Organization's value to gain an advantage with lenders and banks.[[444]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-451)[[445]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-TF-452) He was found liable and ordered to pay nearly $355 million plus interest.[[445]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-TF-452) In August 2025, the appeals court upheld Trump's liability and nonmonetary penalties but voided the monetary penalty as excessive.[[446]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-453)\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Classified_intelligence_material_found_during_search_of_Mar-a-Lago.jpg)Classified intelligence material found during search of Mar-a-Lago\\nIn connection with Trump's efforts to overturn the 2020 election and his involvement in the January 6 attack, in December 2022 the [U.S. House committee on the attack](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_House_Select_Committee_on_the_January_6_Attack \\\"United States House Select Committee on the January 6 Attack\\\") recommended criminal charges against him for [obstructing an official proceeding](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obstructing_an_official_proceeding \\\"Obstructing an official proceeding\\\"), [conspiracy to defraud the United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conspiracy_to_defraud_the_United_States \\\"Conspiracy to defraud the United States\\\"), and inciting or assisting an insurrection.[[447]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-454) In August 2023, a grand jury in [Fulton County, Georgia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fulton_County,_Georgia \\\"Fulton County, Georgia\\\"), indicted him on [13 charges, including racketeering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_election_racketeering_prosecution \\\"Georgia election racketeering prosecution\\\"), for his efforts to subvert the 2020 election in the state.[[448]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-455)[[449]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-456)\\nIn January 2022, the [National Archives and Records Administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Archives_and_Records_Administration \\\"National Archives and Records Administration\\\") retrieved 15 boxes of documents Trump had taken to Mar-a-Lago after leaving the White House, some of which were classified.[[450]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-cnn-tl222-457) In the ensuing Justice Department investigation, officials retrieved more classified documents from his lawyers.[[450]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-cnn-tl222-457) On August 8, 2022, [FBI agents searched Mar-a-Lago](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FBI_search_of_Mar-a-Lago \\\"FBI search of Mar-a-Lago\\\") for illegally held documents, including those in breach of the [Espionage Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Espionage_Act \\\"Espionage Act\\\"), collecting 11 sets of classified documents, some marked top secret.[[451]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-bddj0812222-458)[[452]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-NYT-20220812222-459) A [federal grand jury](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_grand_jury \\\"Federal grand jury\\\") constituted by Special Counsel [Jack Smith](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Smith_\\\\(lawyer\\\\) \\\"Jack Smith \\\\(lawyer\\\\)\\\") indicted Trump in June 2023 on 31 counts of \\\"willfully retaining national defense information\\\" under the [Espionage Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Espionage_Act \\\"Espionage Act\\\"), among other charges.[[450]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-cnn-tl222-457)[[453]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-460)[[454]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-461) Trump pleaded not guilty.[[455]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-462) In July 2024, judge [Aileen Cannon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aileen_Cannon \\\"Aileen Cannon\\\") dismissed [the case](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_\\\\(classified_documents_case\\\\) \\\"Federal prosecution of Donald Trump \\\\(classified documents case\\\\)\\\"), ruling Smith's appointment as special prosecutor was unconstitutional.[[456]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-CD2-463)\\nIn May 2024, Trump [was convicted on 34 felony counts](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_in_New_York \\\"Prosecution of Donald Trump in New York\\\") of [falsifying business records](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falsifying_business_records \\\"Falsifying business records\\\").[[457]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-conv-464) The case stemmed from evidence that he booked [Michael Cohen](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Cohen_\\\\(lawyer\\\\) \\\"Michael Cohen \\\\(lawyer\\\\)\\\")'s hush-money payments to adult film actress [Stormy Daniels](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stormy_Daniels \\\"Stormy Daniels\\\") as business expenses to cover up his alleged 2006–2007 affair with Daniels during the 2016 election.[[457]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-conv-464)[[458]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-465) On January 10, 2025, the judge gave Trump a no-penalty sentence known as an unconditional discharge, saying that punitive requirements would have interfered with presidential immunity.[[459]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-uds-466) After his reelection, the [2020 election obstruction case](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_prosecution_of_Donald_Trump_\\\\(election_obstruction_case\\\\) \\\"Federal prosecution of Donald Trump \\\\(election obstruction case\\\\)\\\") and the classified documents case were dismissed [without prejudice](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prejudice_\\\\(legal_term\\\\)#With_prejudice_and_without_prejudice \\\"Prejudice \\\\(legal term\\\\)\\\") due to Justice Department policy against prosecuting sitting presidents.[[460]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-dismiss2-467)\\n## 2024 presidential election\\nMain article: [2024 United States presidential election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_United_States_presidential_election \\\"2024 United States presidential election\\\")\\nFurther information: [Donald Trump 2024 presidential campaign](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_2024_presidential_campaign \\\"Donald Trump 2024 presidential campaign\\\"), [2024 Republican Party presidential primaries](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Republican_Party_presidential_primaries \\\"2024 Republican Party presidential primaries\\\"), and [Second presidential transition of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidential_transition_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Second presidential transition of Donald Trump\\\")\\nIn November 2022, Trump announced his candidacy for the 2024 presidential election and created a fundraising account.[[461]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-468)[[462]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-469) In March 2023, the campaign began diverting ten percent of the donations to his [leadership PAC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leadership_PAC \\\"Leadership PAC\\\"). His campaign had paid $100 million towards his legal bills by March 2024.[[463]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-470)[[464]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-471)\\nDuring the campaign, Trump made increasingly violent and authoritarian statements.[[465]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-NYT_Authoritarian_Bent-472)[[466]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-473)[[467]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-474)[[468]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-475) He said that he would weaponize the FBI and the Justice Department against his political opponents[[469]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-476)[[470]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-477) and use the military to target Democratic politicians and those that did not support his candidacy.[[471]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-478)[[472]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Lerer_10152024-479) He used harsher and more dehumanizing anti-immigrant rhetoric than during his presidency.[[473]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-480)[[474]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-481)[[475]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-482)[[476]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-483) His rhetoric, calling his political opponents \\\"the enemy\\\", vermin, and fascists, has been described by some historians and scholars as authoritarian, [fascist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_and_fascism \\\"Donald Trump and fascism\\\"),[[h]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Fascist-488) and unlike anything a political candidate has ever said in American history.[[481]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-NYMag_Never_Sounded_Like_This-489)[[472]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Lerer_10152024-479)[[482]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Applebaum18-490) [Age and health concerns](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age_and_health_concerns_about_Donald_Trump \\\"Age and health concerns about Donald Trump\\\") also arose during the campaign, with several medical experts highlighting an increase in rambling, [tangential speech](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangential_speech \\\"Tangential speech\\\") and [behavioral disinhibition](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disinhibition \\\"Disinhibition\\\").[[483]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-491)\\nTrump mentioned \\\"rigged election\\\" and \\\"election interference\\\" earlier and more frequently than in the 2016 and 2020 campaigns and refused to commit to accepting the 2024 election results.[[484]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-492)[[485]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-NYTDoubts-493) Analysts for _The New York Times_ described this as an intensification of his \\\"heads I win; tails you cheated\\\" rhetorical strategy; the newspaper stated that the claim of a rigged election had become the backbone of the campaign.[[485]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-NYTDoubts-493)\\nOn July 13, 2024, Trump was shot in the ear [in an assassination attempt](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempted_assassination_of_Donald_Trump_in_Pennsylvania \\\"Attempted assassination of Donald Trump in Pennsylvania\\\") at a campaign rally in [Butler Township, Pennsylvania](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butler_Township,_Butler_County,_Pennsylvania \\\"Butler Township, Butler County, Pennsylvania\\\").[[486]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Browne-2024-494)[[487]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-495)[[488]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-496) Two days later, the [2024 Republican National Convention](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Republican_National_Convention \\\"2024 Republican National Convention\\\") nominated him as their presidential candidate.[[489]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-497) In September, he was targeted but unharmed in an [assassination attempt in Florida](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempted_assassination_of_Donald_Trump_in_Florida \\\"Attempted assassination of Donald Trump in Florida\\\").[[490]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-498) Trump won the election in November 2024 with 312 electoral votes to incumbent vice president Kamala Harris's 226.[[491]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-499) He also won the popular vote with 49.8% to Harris' 48.3%.[[492]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-500) His victory in 2024 was part of a global backlash against incumbent parties,[[493]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-graveyard-501)[[494]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-502) in large part due to the [2021–2023 inflation surge](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021%E2%80%932023_inflation_surge \\\"2021–2023 inflation surge\\\").[[495]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Global_Politics-503)[[496]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-504) Several outlets described his reelection as an extraordinary comeback.[[497]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-505)[[498]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-506)\\nMain article: [Second presidency of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Second presidency of Donald Trump\\\")\\nFor a chronological guide, see [Timeline of the Donald Trump presidencies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_the_Donald_Trump_presidencies \\\"Timeline of the Donald Trump presidencies\\\").\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Donald_Trump_takes_the_oath_of_office_\\\\(2025\\\\)_\\\\(alternate\\\\).jpg)Taking the [oath of office](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oath_of_office_of_the_President_of_the_United_States \\\"Oath of office of the President of the United States\\\"), administered by [Chief Justice](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_Justice_of_the_United_States \\\"Chief Justice of the United States\\\") [John Roberts](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Roberts \\\"John Roberts\\\"), January 20, 2025\\nTrump began his second term upon [his inauguration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_inauguration_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Second inauguration of Donald Trump\\\") on January 20, 2025.[[499]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-507) He became the [oldest individual to assume the presidency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_presidents_of_the_United_States_by_age \\\"List of presidents of the United States by age\\\"),[[500]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-508) the first president with a felony conviction, and the second person to serve two nonconsecutive terms as president.[[501]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-509)\\nSee also: [First 100 days of the second Trump presidency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_100_days_of_the_second_Trump_presidency \\\"First 100 days of the second Trump presidency\\\")\\nUpon taking office, Trump signed a [series of executive orders](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_executive_orders_in_the_second_Trump_presidency \\\"List of executive orders in the second Trump presidency\\\"). Many of these tested his legal authority, and drew immediate legal action.[[502]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-510) He issued more executive orders on his first day than any other president.[[503]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-511) Four days into his second term, analysis conducted by _[Time](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_\\\\(magazine\\\\) \\\"Time \\\\(magazine\\\\)\\\")_ found that nearly two-thirds of [his executive actions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_executive_actions_by_Donald_Trump \\\"List of executive actions by Donald Trump\\\") \\\"mirror or partially mirror\\\" proposals from [Project 2025](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_2025 \\\"Project 2025\\\").[[504]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-512) In his first weeks, several of his actions ignored or violated federal laws, regulations, and the Constitution according to American legal scholars.[[505]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-513)[[506]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-514)[[507]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-515) In his administration's first month, Trump issued ninety executive orders, memorandums, and directives.[[508]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-516) He used the government to [target his political opponents](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Targeting_of_political_opponents_and_civil_society_under_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Targeting of political opponents and civil society under the second Trump administration\\\") and his actions against [civil society](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_society \\\"Civil society\\\") were described by legal experts and hundreds of political scientists as [authoritarian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Authoritarian \\\"Authoritarian\\\") and contributing to [democratic backsliding](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_backsliding_in_the_United_States \\\"Democratic backsliding in the United States\\\").[[509]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-517)[[510]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-518) His orders and actions were challenged by over 300 lawsuits nationwide, with most of them still moving through the courts by July 2.[[511]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-519)[[512]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-520)\\nFurther information: [Second presidency of Donald Trump § Ethics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Donald_Trump#Ethics \\\"Second presidency of Donald Trump\\\"), and [Cryptocurrency in the second Trump presidency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cryptocurrency_in_the_second_Trump_presidency \\\"Cryptocurrency in the second Trump presidency\\\")\\nTrump's second presidency was described as having fewer guardrails against conflicts of interest than his first,[[513]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Yourish_2025-521) and breaking with decades of ethical norms.[[514]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-522) He maintained a publicly traded company in [Trump Media & Technology Group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Media_%26_Technology_Group \\\"Trump Media & Technology Group\\\"), and diversified it into financial services.[[515]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-523) He pursued new overseas real estate deals involving state-affiliated entities, and had several branding and licensing deals selling Trump-branded merchandise.[[513]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Yourish_2025-521) He profited from events held at his hotels and golf courses and did not place his assets in a blind trust, as previous presidents had done.[[516]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Schouten_1232025-524) In August 2025, Trump's mandatory disclosure of investment showed that, since taking office, he had made 690 purchases of municipal bonds and corporate stock totaling around at least $104 million. Included was stock in companies affected by his changes to federal policies.[[517]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-525)\\nTrump repealed the previous administration's ethics guidelines and enforcement of laws prohibiting bribery[[518]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Weissert_1222025-526)[[519]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-527) without establishing formal ethics guidelines for his political appointees, many of whom entered his administration with an \\\"uncharacteristically large list of potential conflicts of interest\\\".[[520]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-pledges-528) He dropped corruption charges against politicians with ties to him[[521]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-529) and paused enforcement of the law prohibiting companies operating in the U.S. to bribe foreign governments.[[522]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-530)\\nTrump launched, promoted, and personally benefited from [two cryptocurrency tokens](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Trump_Organization#Other_ventures_and_investments \\\"The Trump Organization\\\") (\\\"meme coins\\\"), [$Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/$Trump \\\"$Trump\\\") and $Melania.[[518]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Weissert_1222025-526)[[523]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-531) He also directly benefited from his family's cryptocurrency company [World Liberty Financial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Liberty_Financial \\\"World Liberty Financial\\\"), which engaged in an unprecedented mixing of private enterprise and government policy.[[524]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Lipton_04292025-532)\\nIn July 2025, the Trump administration accepted [a $400 million luxury jet](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P4-HBJ \\\"P4-HBJ\\\") from Qatar to serve as [Air Force One](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_Force_One \\\"Air Force One\\\") until the end of his second term and then be transferred to his presidential library. The retrofitting as Air Force One is estimated to cost up to $1 billion.[[525]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-533)\\n### Mass terminations of federal employees\\nMain article: [2025 United States federal mass layoffs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_United_States_federal_mass_layoffs \\\"2025 United States federal mass layoffs\\\")\\nTrump implemented a hiring freeze across the federal government and ordered telework of federal employees to be discontinued within 30 days.[[526]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-534)[[527]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-upend-535) He ordered a review of many career civil service positions with the intention of [reclassifying them into at-will positions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Policy/Career_appointment \\\"Policy/Career appointment\\\") without job protections.[[527]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-upend-535)[[528]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-536)[[529]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-537) He initiated mass job terminations of federal employees,[[530]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-538) which were described by legal experts as unprecedented or in violation of federal law,[[531]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-539) with the intent of replacing them with workers more aligned with his agenda.[[532]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-540) By late February, the administration had fired more than 30,000 people.[[533]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-541) To facilitate further terminations, it adopted a novel legal interpretation that vastly expands the range of departments and agencies considered as having national security for their primary function,[[534]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-542)[[535]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-543)[[536]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Kanu-2024-544) declaring various federal workers' unions \\\"hostile\\\".[[537]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Davis_O'Brien-2025-545)[[538]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-546) A late March executive order based on this interpretation excluded dozens of departments and agencies from [federal labor-management relations programs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Service_Labor-Management_Relations_Statute \\\"Federal Service Labor-Management Relations Statute\\\"), prompting them to sue to invalidate their [collective bargaining agreements](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collective_bargaining_agreement \\\"Collective bargaining agreement\\\"),[[539]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-547)[[536]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Kanu-2024-544) which could remove union protections from one million federal employees.[[537]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Davis_O'Brien-2025-545)[[540]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-548) He ordered an end to [diversity, equity, and inclusion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diversity,_equity,_and_inclusion \\\"Diversity, equity, and inclusion\\\") (DEI) projects in the federal government and placed employees in DEI offices on leave. He rescinded [Lyndon B. Johnson](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyndon_B._Johnson \\\"Lyndon B. Johnson\\\")'s 1965 [Executive Order 11246](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Executive_Order_11246 \\\"Executive Order 11246\\\"), which mandated that federal contractors take affirmative action to end racial discrimination.[[541]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-549)[[542]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-550)\\nTrump and [Elon Musk](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elon_Musk \\\"Elon Musk\\\")'s [Department of Government Efficiency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Department_of_Government_Efficiency \\\"Department of Government Efficiency\\\") largely dismantled several federal agencies including [USAID](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USAID \\\"USAID\\\") and the [Department of Education](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Education \\\"United States Department of Education\\\"), unilaterally fired several thousand staff, and reduced administrative functions to statutory minimums.[[543]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-551)[[544]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-552)[[545]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-553) Some actions, such as attempts to dismantle the [Consumer Financial Protection Bureau](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consumer_Financial_Protection_Bureau \\\"Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\\\"), were paused by federal courts.[[546]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-554) Many of his actions attempted to bring historically independent institutions under direct executive branch control in diminished forms.[[547]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-555)\\nFurther information: [List of people granted executive clemency in the second Trump presidency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_people_granted_executive_clemency_in_the_second_Trump_presidency \\\"List of people granted executive clemency in the second Trump presidency\\\")\\nAs in his first presidency, Trump frequently bypassed the [Office of the Pardon Attorney](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_of_the_Pardon_Attorney \\\"Office of the Pardon Attorney\\\").[[548]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-556) He installed political loyalist [Ed Martin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ed_Martin_\\\\(Missouri_politician\\\\) \\\"Ed Martin \\\\(Missouri politician\\\\)\\\") who described the rationale for granting pardons as \\\"No MAGA left behind\\\".[[549]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-557) Trump's pardons and grants of clemency favored political allies and loyalists,[[550]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-558)[[551]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Thrush_May292025-559) and disproportionately pardoned \\\"the powerful, famous, well-connected and wealthy\\\" accused of white-collar crime.[[551]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Thrush_May292025-559) Trump [granted clemency to all January 6 rioters](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pardon_of_January_6_United_States_Capitol_attack_defendants \\\"Pardon of January 6 United States Capitol attack defendants\\\") convicted or charged on his first day in office, including those who violently attacked police, by pardoning more than 1,500 and commuting the sentences of 14.[[552]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Reilly2025-560)[[553]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-NPR2025-561)\\nMain articles: [Domestic policy of the second Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domestic_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Domestic policy of the second Trump administration\\\"), [Economic policy of the second Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Economic policy of the second Trump administration\\\"), [Education policy of the second Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Education_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Education policy of the second Trump administration\\\"), and [Science policy of the second Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Science_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Science policy of the second Trump administration\\\")\\nTrump inherited a resilient economy from the Biden administration, with increasing economic growth, low unemployment, and declining inflation.[[i]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-568) He attempted large-scale deregulation by ordering agencies to simply stop enforcement of disfavored rules that legal experts described as illegal and contrary to decades of federal law.[[560]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-569)[[561]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-570) He sought greater government control over private businesses and abandoned traditional conservative [free market](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_market \\\"Free market\\\") orthodoxy,[[562]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-571)[[563]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Morrow_08202025-572)[[564]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Wiles_08222025-573)[[565]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-574) instead favoring [state capitalism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_capitalism \\\"State capitalism\\\") by taking direct government equity stakes in major US corporations.[[566]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-575)[[567]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Economist_08132025-576)[[568]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-577)\\nTrump [canceled and paused federal grants](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_United_States_federal_government_grant_pause \\\"2025 United States federal government grant pause\\\") and made large cuts to scientific research,[[569]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-578) several of which were found by judges and the [Government Accountability Office](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_Accountability_Office \\\"Government Accountability Office\\\") as being illegal and unconstitutional.[[570]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-579)[[571]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-580) He promoted [climate change denial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_change_denial \\\"Climate change denial\\\") and misinformation,[[572]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-581) and appointed oil, gas, and chemical lobbyists to the [EPA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EPA \\\"EPA\\\") to reverse climate regulations and pollution controls.[[573]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-582) Trump relied on declaring national emergencies to justify hundreds of actions and bypass congressional approval or regulatory review.[[574]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-583) For instance, he declared a national energy emergency, allowing the suspension of environmental regulations, loosening the rules for fossil fuel extraction and limiting renewable energy projects.[[575]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-584)[[576]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-585) He initiated a review of the \\\"legality and continued applicability\\\" of the [EPA endangerment finding](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regulation_of_greenhouse_gases_under_the_Clean_Air_Act#EPA_endangerment_findings \\\"Regulation of greenhouse gases under the Clean Air Act\\\"), which is the basis of most federal regulations on greenhouse gases,[[577]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-586) and again withdrew the U.S. from the [Paris Agreement](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris_Agreement \\\"Paris Agreement\\\") on climate change.[[578]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-587)\\nDismantling government agencies enforcing the laws against political corruption and white-collar fraud,[[579]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-588) Trump reduced the Department of Justice's [Public Integrity Section](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_Integrity_Section \\\"Public Integrity Section\\\") from 30 to five lawyers,[[580]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-589)[[581]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-defang-590) dismissed 17 independent [inspectors general](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inspector_general#United_States \\\"Inspector general\\\") at government agencies[[582]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-591) and 12 members of independent oversight boards and watchdog agencies,[[583]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-592) and disbanded the squad in the FBI's Washington field office that investigated allegations of fraud and corruption against government officials and members of Congress.[[584]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-squad-593)[[585]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-594) He pardoned or dropped charges against officials accused of corruption.[[584]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-squad-593)\\nTrump attributed societal problems to [diversity, equity, and inclusion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diversity,_equity,_and_inclusion \\\"Diversity, equity, and inclusion\\\") (DEI) initiatives and [wokeness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woke#United_States \\\"Woke\\\").[[586]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Green250203-595) Equating diversity with incompetence,[[586]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Green250203-595) he reversed pro-diversity policies in the federal government.[[587]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-596)[[588]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-597) His administration aggressively moved against the rights of transgender people and what it termed \\\"[gender ideology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gender_ideology \\\"Gender ideology\\\")\\\".[[589]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-598)[[590]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-599) He sought to remake civil society to his preferences by executive order.[[591]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-APfirms-600) On DEI and antisemitism grounds, he threatened cultural institutions[[592]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-601) and sixty universities,[[593]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-WSJed-602) and forced law firms to capitulate to his political agenda.[[591]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-APfirms-600)\\n#### One Big Beautiful Bill Act\\nIn July 2025, Trump signed the [One Big Beautiful Bill Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_Big_Beautiful_Bill_Act \\\"One Big Beautiful Bill Act\\\") into law. The bill made the tax cuts of the 2017 [Tax Cuts and Jobs Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tax_Cuts_and_Jobs_Act \\\"Tax Cuts and Jobs Act\\\") permanent and added additional tax deductions for a total of around $4.5 trillion, mostly benefiting the highest income brackets and costing people in the lowest income bracket $1,600 per year; increased funding for national defense, deportations, the border wall, and a missile shield; and removed tax credits for clean energy projects using renewable energy sources such as wind and solar power and for buyers of electric vehicles. The bill cut funding for [Medicaid](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medicaid \\\"Medicaid\\\") and [SNAP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supplemental_Nutrition_Assistance_Program \\\"Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program\\\") and added additional work requirements for eligibility and a $35 co-payment for some Medicaid services; the cuts and additional requirements will take effect after the 2026 general election. The bill was projected by the [Congressional Budget Office](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congressional_Budget_Office \\\"Congressional Budget Office\\\") to increase the budget deficit by $3.4 trillion by 2034, cause 11.8 million people to lose Medicaid coverage, and eliminate SNAP benefits for three million people.[[594]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-603)[[595]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-604)\\nMain articles: [Immigration policy of the second Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immigration_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Immigration policy of the second Trump administration\\\") and [Deportation in the second Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deportation_in_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Deportation in the second Trump administration\\\")\\nFurther information: [Mexico–United States border crisis § Second Trump administration (2025–present)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico%E2%80%93United_States_border_crisis#Second_Trump_administration_\\\\(2025%E2%80%93present\\\\) \\\"Mexico–United States border crisis\\\"), and [Mexico–United States border wall § Second Trump administration (2025–present)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico%E2%80%93United_States_border_wall#Second_Trump_administration_\\\\(2025%E2%80%93present\\\\) \\\"Mexico–United States border wall\\\")\\nIn his first days in office, Trump instructed Border Patrol agents to summarily deport [illegal immigrants](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illegal_immigration_to_the_United_States \\\"Illegal immigration to the United States\\\") crossing the border, disabling the [CBP One](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBP_One \\\"CBP One\\\") app that was being used to schedule border crossings. He resumed the [remain in Mexico](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remain_in_Mexico \\\"Remain in Mexico\\\") policy, designated drug cartels as terrorist groups, and ordered construction to be resumed on a border wall.[[596]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-605)[[597]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Cbsjan28-606)\\nDeportation operations first focused on \\\"target lists\\\" of criminals formed prior to Trump's second term.[[598]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-607) Then his administration removed asylum applicants who failed to meet requirements,[[599]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-AxiosJan20-608) revoked the parole status of immigrants who entered the U.S. under CBP One and [CHNV](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parole_for_Cubans,_Haitians,_Nicaraguans,_and_Venezuelans \\\"Parole for Cubans, Haitians, Nicaraguans, and Venezuelans\\\") humanitarian parole,[[597]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Cbsjan28-606) attempted to remove [birthright citizenship](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birthright_citizenship_in_the_United_States \\\"Birthright citizenship in the United States\\\"),[[600]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-609) and suspended the [Refugee Admissions Program](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Refugee_Admissions_Program \\\"United States Refugee Admissions Program\\\").[[601]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-610) In March, he used the [Alien Enemies Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_Enemies_Act \\\"Alien Enemies Act\\\") of 1798 to [imprison illegal immigrants without trial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/March_2025_American_deportations_of_Venezuelans \\\"March 2025 American deportations of Venezuelans\\\")—one by \\\"administrative error\\\"[[602]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-611) and most without criminal records[[j]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-613)—at the [Terrorism Confinement Center](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terrorism_Confinement_Center \\\"Terrorism Confinement Center\\\") in El Salvador.[[604]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-614) [He targeted activists](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Activist_deportations_in_the_second_Trump_presidency \\\"Activist deportations in the second Trump presidency\\\"), legal immigrants, tourists, and students with visas who expressed criticism of his policies or pro-Palestinian advocacy.[[605]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-615)\\nMain article: [Foreign policy of the second Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_policy_of_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Foreign policy of the second Trump administration\\\")\\nFurther information: [Tariffs in the second Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariffs_in_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Tariffs in the second Trump administration\\\"), [China–United States trade war](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China%E2%80%93United_States_trade_war \\\"China–United States trade war\\\"), [2025 United States trade war with Canada and Mexico](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_United_States_trade_war_with_Canada_and_Mexico \\\"2025 United States trade war with Canada and Mexico\\\"), and [American expansionism under Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_expansionism_under_Donald_Trump \\\"American expansionism under Donald Trump\\\")\\nTrump's second term foreign policy has been variously described as [imperialist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_imperialism \\\"American imperialism\\\"), [expansionist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expansionist \\\"Expansionist\\\"),[[606]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-616)[[607]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-617) [isolationist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isolationist \\\"Isolationist\\\"), and [autarkist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autarky \\\"Autarky\\\"), employing the \\\"[America First](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/America_First \\\"America First\\\")\\\" ideology as its cornerstone.[[608]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Blaxland_2182025-618) His relations with allies were transactional and ranged from indifference to hostility, including threats of annexation.[[609]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Erlanger_2272025-619)[[610]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Broadwater_2272025-620) He ordered the U.S. government to stop funding and working with the WHO and announced the U.S.'s [intention to formally leave the WHO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Withdrawing_the_United_States_from_the_World_Health_Organization \\\"Withdrawing the United States from the World Health Organization\\\").[[611]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-621)[[612]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-622)\\nMeeting with Zelenskyy (49:21)\\nIn February 2025, Trump and Vice President Vance [met with Volodymyr Zelenskyy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_Trump%E2%80%93Zelenskyy_Oval_Office_meeting \\\"2025 Trump–Zelenskyy Oval Office meeting\\\"), the president of Ukraine, in the [Oval Office](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oval_Office \\\"Oval Office\\\"). The meeting, which was televised live, was highly contentious as Trump and Vance berated Zelenskyy. Media outlets described it as an unprecedented public confrontation between an American president and a foreign [head of state](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_of_state \\\"Head of state\\\").[[613]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Liptak_20250228-623)[[614]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-624)\\nTrump and his incoming administration helped broker [a Gaza war ceasefire](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_Gaza_war_ceasefire \\\"2025 Gaza war ceasefire\\\") alongside the Biden administration, enacted a day prior to his inauguration.[[615]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-625)[[616]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-626)[[617]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-credit-627) In March, Israel broke the ceasefire.[[617]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-credit-627)\\nTrump's economic policies have been described as [protectionist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protectionism_in_the_United_States \\\"Protectionism in the United States\\\"),[[618]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-628) with Trump imposing tariffs on most countries, including large tariffs on major trading partners China, Canada, and Mexico.[[619]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-629) He started a global [trade war](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_war \\\"Trade war\\\"), imposing tariffs at the highest level since the 1930 [Smoot–Hawley Tariff Act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoot%E2%80%93Hawley_Tariff_Act \\\"Smoot–Hawley Tariff Act\\\") at the onset of the [Great Depression](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Depression \\\"Great Depression\\\").[[620]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-630)[[621]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-631) Economists argued that the administration misunderstood the relationship between [trade deficits](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balance_of_trade \\\"Balance of trade\\\") and tariffs, using flawed assumptions.[[622]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-632) He suspended American financial contributions to the [World Trade Organization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Trade_Organization \\\"World Trade Organization\\\").[[623]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-633)\\nMain articles: [Political appointments of the second Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_appointments_of_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Political appointments of the second Trump administration\\\") and [Second cabinet of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_cabinet_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Second cabinet of Donald Trump\\\")\\nIn his second term, Trump selected [cabinet](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_cabinet_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Second cabinet of Donald Trump\\\") members with personal loyalty to him,[[624]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-634)[[625]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-focus-635) with the \\\"focus on loyalty over subject-matter expertise\\\".[[625]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-focus-635) In February 2025, the White House stated that [Elon Musk](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elon_Musk \\\"Elon Musk\\\") was a [special government employee](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special_government_employee \\\"Special government employee\\\").[[626]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-musk-636) Trump gave Musk's [Department of Government Efficiency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Department_of_Government_Efficiency \\\"Department of Government Efficiency\\\") (DOGE) access to many federal government agencies.[[626]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-musk-636) Musk's teams operated in eighteen departments and agencies in the administration's first month,[[627]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-637) including in the Treasury Department's $5 trillion payment system,[[628]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-638) the [Small Business Administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_Business_Administration \\\"Small Business Administration\\\"), the [Office of Personnel Management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_of_Personnel_Management \\\"Office of Personnel Management\\\"), and the [General Services Administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Services_Administration \\\"General Services Administration\\\").[[629]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-639) He nominated or appointed 23 former [Fox News](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fox_News \\\"Fox News\\\") employees to his administration.[[630]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-640)\\nSee also: [List of federal judges appointed by Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_federal_judges_appointed_by_Donald_Trump \\\"List of federal judges appointed by Donald Trump\\\")\\nFederal judges found many of the administration's actions to be illegal.[[631]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-641) Following legal setbacks, Trump increased his criticism of the judiciary and called for impeachment of federal judges who ruled against him.[[632]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-642) He threatened, signed executive actions, and ordered investigations into his political opponents, critics, and organizations aligned with the Democratic Party.[[633]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-643) By mid-July, a _[Washington Post](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington_Post \\\"Washington Post\\\")_ analysis found he defied judges and the courts in roughly one third of all cases against him, actions which were described by legal experts as unprecedented for any presidential administration.[[634]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-644) His defiance of court orders and a claimed right to disobey the courts raised fears among legal experts of a [constitutional crisis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitutional_crisis \\\"Constitutional crisis\\\").[[635]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-645)\\nMultiple analyses conducted by academic scholars and _[The New York Times](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times \\\"The New York Times\\\")_ found that both Republican and Democratic judicial appointees have found numerous constitutional and statutory flaws with Trump administration policies.[[636]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-646) His administration asserted a constitutional right to ignore federal law in its justification of several actions such as in his refusal to enforce a [federal ban on TikTok](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protecting_Americans_from_Foreign_Adversary_Controlled_Applications_Act \\\"Protecting Americans from Foreign Adversary Controlled Applications Act\\\"), with legal experts describing it as claiming a \\\"constitutional power to immunize private parties to commit otherwise illegal acts with impunity\\\".[[637]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-647) He also engaged in an unprecedented [targeting of law firms and lawyers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Targeting_of_law_firms_and_lawyers_under_the_second_Trump_administration \\\"Targeting of law firms and lawyers under the second Trump administration\\\") that previously represented positions adverse to himself.[[638]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-648)[[639]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-649)\\n## Political practice and rhetoric\\nFurther information: [Trumpism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trumpism \\\"Trumpism\\\"), [Political positions of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_positions_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Political positions of Donald Trump\\\"), and [Rhetoric of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhetoric_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Rhetoric of Donald Trump\\\")\\nBeginning with his 2016 campaign, Trump's politics and rhetoric led to the creation of a [political movement](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_movement \\\"Political movement\\\") known as Trumpism.[[640]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-650) His political positions are [populist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Populist \\\"Populist\\\"),[[641]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTERoss2024298\\\"In_2016,_a_populist_won_the_presidential_election_in_the_United_States.\\\"-651)[[642]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEUrbinati2019-652) more specifically described as [right-wing populist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right-wing_populist \\\"Right-wing populist\\\").[[643]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTECampaniConcepci%C3%B3nSolerSav%C3%ADn2022-653)[[644]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-654) He has been described as embracing [far-right extremism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Far-right_extremism \\\"Far-right extremism\\\"),[[645]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Baker_07272025-655)[[646]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-656) and he helped bring far-right fringe ideas and organizations into the mainstream.[[647]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-657) Many of his actions and rhetoric have been described as [authoritarian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Authoritarian \\\"Authoritarian\\\") and contributing to [democratic backsliding](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_backsliding_in_the_United_States \\\"Democratic backsliding in the United States\\\").[[648]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEParkerTowler2019505;_513\\\"The_rise_of_Donald_Trump,_however,_forces_scholars_to_apprehend_why_the_country_is_trending_toward_authoritarianism,_complete_with_a_renegade_executive_and_party_loyalists_willing_to_permit_him_to_govern_as_he_sees_fit._Again,_this_is_not_the_first_time_the_United_States_has_confronted_authoritarianism._..._We_must_also_say_something_about_the_much-discussed_topic_of_authoritarianism_and_the_election_of_Donald_Trump._By_now,_several_books,_including_''How_Democracies_Die'',_have_identified_Trump_as_an_authoritarian.\\\"-658)[[649]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKaufmanHaggard2019-659) Trump pushed for an expansion of presidential power under a maximalist interpretation of the [unitary executive theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unitary_executive_theory \\\"Unitary executive theory\\\").[[650]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-660)[[651]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-661) His political base [has been compared](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trumpism#Cult_of_personality \\\"Trumpism\\\") to a [cult of personality](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cult_of_personality \\\"Cult of personality\\\").[[k]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Cult-669)\\nTrump's rhetoric and actions have been accused of creating and exacerbating anger and distrust through the use of an [\\\"us\\\" versus \\\"them\\\" narrative](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In-group_and_out-group \\\"In-group and out-group\\\").[[659]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTERoss2024299\\\"Through_his_rhetoric_and_action,_Trump_inflamed_anger_and_exacerbated_distrust_in_a_way_that_deepened_the_divide_between_the_\\\"us\\\"_and_the_\\\"them\\\"\\\"-670) He explicitly and routinely disparages racial, religious, and ethnic minorities,[[660]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStephens-Dougan2021302\\\"Trump,_however,_managed_to_achieve_electoral_success_in_2016_despite_routinely_using_racial_appeals_that_openly_and_categorically_disparaged_racial,_religious,_and_ethnic_minorities,_or_what_the_racial_priming_literature_refers_to_as_explicit_racial_appeals._..._Throughout_his_campaign_and_subsequent_presidency,_Trump_continued_to_traffic_in_similar_explicit_racial_appeals\\\"-671) and scholars consistently find that racial animus regarding blacks, immigrants, and Muslims are the best predictors of support for Trump.[[661]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBerman202176\\\"In_the_United,_States_scholars_consistently_find_that_\\\"racial_animus,\\\"_or_attitudes_regarding_\\\"blacks,_immigrants,_Muslims\\\"_are_the_best_predictors_of_support_for_President_Trump\\\"-672) His rhetoric has been described as using [fearmongering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fearmongering \\\"Fearmongering\\\") and [demagogy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demagogy \\\"Demagogy\\\") which [intensified during his 2024 presidential campaign](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_2024_presidential_campaign#Fearmongering \\\"Donald Trump 2024 presidential campaign\\\").[[662]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-673) He has said that he believes real power comes from fear.[[663]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-674) The [alt-right](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alt-right \\\"Alt-right\\\") movement coalesced around and supported his candidacy, due in part to its [opposition to multiculturalism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opposition_to_multiculturalism \\\"Opposition to multiculturalism\\\") and [immigration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opposition_to_immigration \\\"Opposition to immigration\\\").[[664]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-675)[[665]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-676)[[666]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-677) He has a strong [appeal to evangelical Christian voters](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trumpism#Christian_Trumpism \\\"Trumpism\\\") and [Christian nationalists](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christian_nationalism#United_States \\\"Christian nationalism\\\"),[[667]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEPerryWhiteheadGrubbs2021229-678) and his rallies take on the symbols, rhetoric, and agenda of Christian nationalism.[[668]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-679) Trump has also used [anti-communist sentiment](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-communism \\\"Anti-communism\\\") in his rhetoric, regularly calling his opponents \\\"[communists](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communism \\\"Communism\\\")\\\" and \\\"[Marxists](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marxism \\\"Marxism\\\")\\\".[[669]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-680)[[670]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-681)\\n### Racial and gender views\\nMain articles: [Racial views of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racial_views_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Racial views of Donald Trump\\\") and [Donald Trump sexual misconduct allegations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_sexual_misconduct_allegations \\\"Donald Trump sexual misconduct allegations\\\")\\nMany of Trump's comments and actions have been characterized as racist.[[671]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-682) In a 2018 national poll, about half of respondents said he is racist; a greater proportion believed that he emboldened racists.[[672]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-683) Several studies and surveys found that racist attitudes fueled his political ascent and were more important than economic factors in determining the allegiance of Trump voters.[[673]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-684) Racist and [Islamophobic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamophobic \\\"Islamophobic\\\") attitudes are strong indicators of support for Trump.[[674]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTELajevardiOskooii2018-685) He has been accused of racism for insisting a group of five black and Latino teenagers were guilty of raping a white woman in the 1989 [Central Park jogger case](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Park_jogger_case \\\"Central Park jogger case\\\"), even after they were exonerated in 2002.[[675]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-686)\\nIn 2011, Trump became the leading proponent of the racist [\\\"birther\\\" conspiracy theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barack_Obama_citizenship_conspiracy_theories \\\"Barack Obama citizenship conspiracy theories\\\") that Barack Obama, the first black U.S. president, was not born in the United States.[[676]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-687) He claimed credit for pressuring the government to publish Obama's birth certificate, which he considered fraudulent.[[677]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-688) He acknowledged that Obama was born in the U.S. in September 2016,[[678]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-689) though reportedly expressed birther views privately in 2017.[[679]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-690) During the 2024 presidential campaign, he made false attacks against the racial identity of his opponent, Kamala Harris, that were described as reminiscent of the birther conspiracy theory.[[680]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-691) His 2024 campaign made extensive use of [dehumanizing language](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dehumanization \\\"Dehumanization\\\") and [racial stereotypes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethnic_stereotype \\\"Ethnic stereotype\\\").[[681]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-692)[[682]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-693)[[683]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-694)[[684]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-695) In 2025, he promoted false claims of [white genocide](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_genocide \\\"White genocide\\\") in South Africa[[685]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-696) and created the [White South African refugee program](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_South_African_refugee_program \\\"White South African refugee program\\\").[[686]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-697)\\nTrump has a history of belittling women when speaking to the media and on social media.[[687]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTERotheCollins2019-698)[[688]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Shear-699) He made lewd comments, disparaged women's physical appearances, and referred to them using derogatory epithets.[[688]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Shear-699) As of 2020, 26 women have publicly [accused him of sexual misconduct](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_sexual_misconduct_allegations \\\"Donald Trump sexual misconduct allegations\\\"), including rape, kissing without consent, groping, looking under women's skirts, and walking in on naked teenage pageant contestants. He has denied the allegations.[[689]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-no26-700) In October 2016, a 2005 \\\"[hot mic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_mic \\\"Hot mic\\\")\\\" recording surfaced in which [he bragged](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_Access_Hollywood_tape \\\"Donald Trump Access Hollywood tape\\\") about kissing and groping women without their consent, saying that, \\\"when you're a star, they let you do it. You can do anything. ... Grab 'em by the [pussy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pussy#Female_genitalia \\\"Pussy\\\").\\\"[[690]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-701) He characterized the comments as \\\"locker-room talk\\\".[[691]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-702)[[692]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-703) The incident's widespread media exposure led to his first public apology, videotaped during his 2016 presidential campaign.[[693]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-704)\\n### Link to violence and hate crimes\\nFurther information: [Rhetoric of Donald Trump § Violence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhetoric_of_Donald_Trump#Violence \\\"Rhetoric of Donald Trump\\\")\\nTrump has been identified as a key figure in increasing political violence in the U.S., both for and against him.[[694]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-705)[[695]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTENacosShapiroBloch-Elkon2020-706)[[696]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEPiazzaVan_Doren2022-707) He is described as embracing extremism, [conspiracy theories](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conspiracy_theories_promoted_by_Donald_Trump \\\"List of conspiracy theories promoted by Donald Trump\\\") such as [Q-Anon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Q-Anon \\\"Q-Anon\\\"), and far-right [militia movements](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_militia_movement \\\"American militia movement\\\") to a greater extent than any modern American president,[[645]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Baker_07272025-655)[[697]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-708) and engaging in [stochastic terrorism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stochastic_terrorism \\\"Stochastic terrorism\\\").[[698]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-709)[[699]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-710)\\nResearch suggests Trump's rhetoric is associated with an increased incidence of hate crimes,[[700]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-711)[[701]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-712) and that he has an emboldening effect on expressing prejudicial attitudes due to his normalization of explicit racial rhetoric.[[702]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStephens-Dougan2021306\\\"The_election_of_President_Donald_Trump,_however,_indicates_that_a_candidate_who_utilizes_explicit_racial_rhetoric_is_not_necessarily_penalized._In_fact,_some_research_suggests_that_Trump's_2016_presidential_campaign_may_have_had_an_emboldening_effect,_such_that_some_voters_felt_more_comfortable_expressing_prejudicial_attitudes_because_of_Trump's_normalization_of_racist_rhetoric\\\"-713) During his 2016 campaign, he urged or praised physical attacks against protesters or reporters.[[703]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-714)[[704]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-715) Numerous defendants investigated or prosecuted for violent acts and hate crimes cited his rhetoric in arguing that they were not culpable or should receive leniency.[[705]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-716)[[706]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-717) A nationwide review by [ABC News](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_News_\\\\(United_States\\\\) \\\"ABC News \\\\(United States\\\\)\\\") in May 2020 identified at least 54 criminal cases, from August 2015 to April 2020, in which he was invoked in direct connection with violence or threats of violence mostly by white men and primarily against minorities.[[707]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-718) Trump's refusal to condemn the white supremacist [Proud Boys](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proud_Boys \\\"Proud Boys\\\") during a 2020 presidential debate[[708]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-719) and his comment, \\\"Proud Boys, stand back and stand by\\\", were said to have led to increased recruitment for the pro-Trump group.[[709]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-720) Counterterrorism researchers described his normalization and [revisionist history](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historical_revisionism \\\"Historical revisionism\\\") of the January 6 Capitol attack, and [grant of clemency to all January 6 rioters](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pardon_of_January_6_United_States_Capitol_attack_defendants \\\"Pardon of January 6 United States Capitol attack defendants\\\"), as encouraging future political violence.[[710]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-721)[[711]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-722)\\n### Conspiracy theories\\nMain article: [List of conspiracy theories promoted by Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_conspiracy_theories_promoted_by_Donald_Trump \\\"List of conspiracy theories promoted by Donald Trump\\\")\\nSince before his first presidency, Trump has promoted numerous conspiracy theories, including Obama \\\"birtherism\\\", [climate change denial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_change_denial \\\"Climate change denial\\\"), and [alleged Ukrainian interference in U.S. elections](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conspiracy_theories_related_to_the_Trump%E2%80%93Ukraine_scandal \\\"Conspiracy theories related to the Trump–Ukraine scandal\\\").[[712]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-723)[[713]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Haberman2016-724)[[714]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-725) After the 2020 presidential election, he promoted conspiracy theories for his defeat that were characterized as \\\"[the big lie](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_lie#Donald_Trump's_false_claims_of_a_stolen_election \\\"Big lie\\\")\\\".[[715]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-726)[[716]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-727)\\n### False or misleading statements\\nMain article: [False or misleading statements by Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/False_or_misleading_statements_by_Donald_Trump \\\"False or misleading statements by Donald Trump\\\")\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2017-_Donald_Trump_veracity_-_composite_graph.png)[Fact-checkers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fact-checkers \\\"Fact-checkers\\\") from _The Washington Post_ ,[[717]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-database-728) the _Toronto Star_ ,[[718]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-729) and CNN[[719]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-730) compiled data on \\\"false or misleading claims\\\" (orange background) and \\\"false claims\\\" (violet foreground).\\nTrump frequently makes false statements in public remarks[[720]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-finnegan-731)[[147]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-whoppers-151) to an extent unprecedented in [American politics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_politics \\\"American politics\\\").[[720]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-finnegan-731)[[721]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-glasser-732)[[722]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Konnikova-733) His falsehoods are a distinctive part of his political identity[[721]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-glasser-732) and have been described as [firehosing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firehosing \\\"Firehosing\\\").[[723]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKakutani201894%E2%80%93104-734) His false and misleading statements were documented by [fact-checkers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fact-checker \\\"Fact-checker\\\"), including at _The Washington Post_ , which tallied 30,573 false or misleading statements made by him during his first presidency,[[717]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-database-728) increasing in frequency over time.[[724]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-TermUntruth-735)\\nSome of Trump's falsehoods were inconsequential,[[725]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-736)[[726]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-737) while others had more far-reaching effects, such as his unproven promotion of antimalarial drugs as a treatment for COVID-19,[[727]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-738)[[728]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-739) causing a U.S. shortage of these drugs and [panic-buying](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panic-buying \\\"Panic-buying\\\") in Africa and South Asia.[[729]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-740)[[730]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-741) Other misinformation, such as misattributing a rise in crime in [England and Wales](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/England_and_Wales \\\"England and Wales\\\") to the \\\"spread of radical Islamic terror\\\", served his domestic political purposes.[[731]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-742) His attacks on mail-in ballots and other election practices weakened public faith in the integrity of the 2020 presidential election,[[732]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-743)[[733]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-744) while his disinformation about the pandemic delayed and weakened the national response to it.[[734]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-NYT_4_11_20-745)[[735]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-USAT-Disinfo-746)[[736]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-747) He habitually does not apologize for his falsehoods.[[737]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-748) Until 2018, the media rarely referred to his falsehoods as lies, including when he repeated demonstrably false statements.[[738]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-749)[[739]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-DBauder-750)[[740]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-751)\\n### Social media\\nMain articles: [Social media use by Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_media_use_by_Donald_Trump \\\"Social media use by Donald Trump\\\") and [Twitter use by Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twitter_use_by_Donald_Trump \\\"Twitter use by Donald Trump\\\")\\nSee also: [Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump § Lawsuits over social media ban](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_and_business_legal_affairs_of_Donald_Trump#Lawsuits_over_social_media_ban \\\"Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump\\\")\\nTrump's social media presence attracted worldwide attention after he joined Twitter in 2009. He posted frequently during his 2016 campaign and as president until [Twitter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twitter \\\"Twitter\\\") banned him after the January 6 attack.[[741]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-752) He often used Twitter to communicate directly with the public and sideline the press;[[742]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-gone-753) in 2017, his press secretary said that his [tweets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tweet_\\\\(social_media\\\\) \\\"Tweet \\\\(social media\\\\)\\\") constituted official presidential statements.[[743]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-754)\\nAfter years of criticism for allowing Trump to post misinformation and falsehoods, Twitter began to tag some of his tweets with fact-checks in May 2020.[[744]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-755) In response, he said social media platforms \\\"totally silence\\\" conservatives and he would \\\"strongly regulate, or close them down\\\".[[745]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-756) After the January 6 attack, he was banned from [Facebook](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook \\\"Facebook\\\"), [Instagram](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instagram \\\"Instagram\\\"), Twitter, and other platforms.[[746]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-757) The loss of his social media presence diminished his ability to shape events[[747]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-758)[[748]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-759) and correlated with a dramatic decrease in the volume of misinformation on Twitter.[[749]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-760) In February 2022, he launched social media platform [Truth Social](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truth_Social \\\"Truth Social\\\") where he attracted a fraction of his Twitter following.[[750]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-761) [Elon Musk](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elon_Musk \\\"Elon Musk\\\"), after [acquiring Twitter](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acquisition_of_Twitter_by_Elon_Musk \\\"Acquisition of Twitter by Elon Musk\\\"), reinstated his Twitter account in November 2022.[[751]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-762)[[752]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-763) [Meta Platforms](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meta_Platforms \\\"Meta Platforms\\\")' two-year ban lapsed in January 2023, allowing him to return to Facebook and Instagram.[[753]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-764)\\n### Relationship with the press\\nFurther information: [First presidency of Donald Trump § Relationship with the news media](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_presidency_of_Donald_Trump#Relationship_with_the_news_media \\\"First presidency of Donald Trump\\\"), [Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_and_business_legal_affairs_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Personal and business legal affairs of Donald Trump\\\"), and [Donald Trump's conflict with the media](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump%27s_conflict_with_the_media \\\"Donald Trump's conflict with the media\\\")\\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:President_Trump%27s_First_100_Days-_45_\\\\(33573172373\\\\).jpg)Talking to the press, March 2017\\nTrump sought media attention throughout his career, maintaining a \\\"love-hate\\\" relationship with the press.[[754]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-765) In the 2016 campaign, he benefited from a record amount of free media coverage,[[755]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Cillizza-160614-766) estimated at $2 billion.[[756]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-767) As a candidate and as president, he frequently accused the press of bias, calling it the \\\"fake news media\\\" and \\\"the [enemy of the people](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enemy_of_the_people \\\"Enemy of the people\\\")\\\".[[757]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-768)\\nThe first Trump presidency reduced formal press briefings from about one hundred in 2017 to about half that in 2018 and to two in 2019; they also revoked the press passes of two White House reporters, which were restored by the courts.[[758]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-769) Trump's 2020 presidential campaign sued _The New York Times_ , _The Washington Post_ , and CNN for defamation in opinion pieces about his stance on Russian election interference. All the suits were dismissed.[[759]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-770)[[760]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Atlantic_Press-771) By 2024, Trump repeatedly voiced support for [outlawing political dissent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Criminalization_of_dissent \\\"Criminalization of dissent\\\") and criticism,[[761]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-772) and said that reporters should be prosecuted for not divulging confidential sources and media companies should possibly lose their broadcast licenses for unfavorable coverage of him.[[762]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-773)\\nFollowing his reelection, Trump launched lawsuits and created blacklists against certain media outlets, and took over the process run by the [White House Correspondents' Association](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_Correspondents%27_Association \\\"White House Correspondents' Association\\\") to choose what outlets have access to him.[[763]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-774) Rather than comply with a court order to restore [Associated Press](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associated_Press \\\"Associated Press\\\") access, the White House made a policy of limited access for all wire services.[[764]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-775) The [Federal Communications Commission](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Communications_Commission \\\"Federal Communications Commission\\\") launched investigations into media outlets accused of bias against him.[[765]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-776)\\n## Personal life\\n### Family\\nFurther information: [Trump family](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_family \\\"Trump family\\\")\\nIn 1977, Trump married Ivana.[[766]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBlair2015300-777) They had three children: [Donald Jr.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Jr. \\\"Donald Jr.\\\") (b. 1977), [Ivanka](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivanka_Trump \\\"Ivanka Trump\\\") (b. 1981), and [Eric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Trump \\\"Eric Trump\\\") (b. 1984). The couple divorced in 1990, following his affair with model and actress [Marla Maples](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marla_Maples \\\"Marla Maples\\\").[[767]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-778) He and Maples married in 1993 and divorced in 1999. They have one daughter, [Tiffany](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiffany_Trump \\\"Tiffany Trump\\\") (b. 1993), whom Maples raised in California.[[768]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-779) In 2005, he married Slovenian model [Melania Knauss](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melania_Knauss \\\"Melania Knauss\\\").[[769]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017266-780) They have one son, [Barron](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barron_Trump \\\"Barron Trump\\\") (b. 2006).[[770]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-781)\\n### Relationship with Jeffrey Epstein\\nTrump had a 15-year friendship with Jeffrey Epstein; persons who knew them at the time said they frequently hit on and competed for women.[[771]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-782) Media attention and public pressure mounted in 2025, when his administration did not release [files relating to Epstein](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeffrey_Epstein_client_list \\\"Jeffrey Epstein client list\\\"), despite Trump's promise to do so during the 2024 campaign.[[772]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-783)\\n### Health\\nMain article: [Age and health concerns about Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age_and_health_concerns_about_Donald_Trump \\\"Age and health concerns about Donald Trump\\\")\\nTrump says he has never drunk alcohol, smoked cigarettes, or used drugs.[[773]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-784)[[774]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-785) He sleeps about four or five hours a night.[[775]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-786)[[776]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEAlmondDu2020-787) He has called golfing his \\\"primary form of exercise\\\", but usually does not walk the course.[[777]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-788) He considers exercise a waste of energy because he believes the body is \\\"like a battery, with a finite amount of energy\\\", which is depleted by exercise.[[778]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-789)[[779]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEO'DonnellRutherford1991133-790) In 2015, his campaign released a letter from his longtime personal physician, [Harold Bornstein](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harold_Bornstein \\\"Harold Bornstein\\\"), stating that he would \\\"be the healthiest individual ever elected to the presidency\\\".[[780]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-dictation-791) In 2018, Bornstein said Trump had dictated the contents of the letter and that three of Trump's agents had seized his medical records in a February 2017 raid on Bornstein's office.[[780]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-dictation-791)[[781]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-792)\\n### Religion\\nMain article: [Donald Trump and religion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_and_religion \\\"Donald Trump and religion\\\")\\nTrump said in 2016 that he was a [Presbyterian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presbyterian \\\"Presbyterian\\\") and a [Protestant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protestant \\\"Protestant\\\").[[782]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-793)[[783]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-794) In 2020, he said he was a [nondenominational Christian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nondenominational_Christian \\\"Nondenominational Christian\\\").[[784]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-795) However, many have questioned the depth of these religious affiliations. A survey during [Trump's first presidency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump%27s_first_presidency \\\"Trump's first presidency\\\") (2017–2021) showed that 63 percent of Americans did not believe he was religious, despite his professed Christian affiliation, and that only 44 percent of Americans believed that Trump was a Christian.[[785]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-796) Some of Trump's comments on the Bible or Christian practice have led critical observers to suggest that his knowledge of Christianity is superficial or erroneous, and few biographers have described Trump as deeply or even particularly religious.[[786]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-LATimes17-797)[[787]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-NPR16-798)\\nIn his first term, Trump appointed his personal pastor[[788]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-799) and spiritual advisor,[[789]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-800) millionaire televangelist [Paula White-Cain](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paula_White-Cain \\\"Paula White-Cain\\\"), to the White House [Office of Public Liaison](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Office_of_Public_Liaison \\\"Office of Public Liaison\\\").[[790]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-801) In his second term, he appointed her senior advisor to the newly created [White House Faith Office](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House_Faith_Office \\\"White House Faith Office\\\").[[791]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-802)[[792]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-803)\\n## Assessments\\n### Public image\\nMain articles: [Public image of Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_image_of_Donald_Trump \\\"Public image of Donald Trump\\\") and [Donald Trump in popular culture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump_in_popular_culture \\\"Donald Trump in popular culture\\\")\\nSee also: [Opinion polling on the first Donald Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opinion_polling_on_the_first_Donald_Trump_administration \\\"Opinion polling on the first Donald Trump administration\\\") and [Opinion polling on the second Donald Trump administration](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opinion_polling_on_the_second_Donald_Trump_administration \\\"Opinion polling on the second Donald Trump administration\\\")\\nIn Trump's first term, from 2017 to 2020, international approval ratings of U.S. leadership dropped from about 22 percent in a [Gallup](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gallup,_Inc. \\\"Gallup, Inc.\\\") poll[[793]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-804) of 134 countries to just 16 percent—lower than China's [Xi Jinping](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xi_Jinping \\\"Xi Jinping\\\") and Russia's [Vladimir Putin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_Putin \\\"Vladimir Putin\\\")—in a [Pew Research](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pew_Research \\\"Pew Research\\\") poll[[794]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-805) of 13 countries. In 2017, estimation of U.S. leadership declined most among allies.[[795]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-806)\\nDomestically, in his first term, Trump had chiefly partisan support: 88 percent among Republicans and 7 percent among Democrats.[[796]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Jones-807) In a 2021 Gallup poll, he was the only president never to reach a 50 percent approval rating, and he was the first not to be named most admired in his first year in office.[[797]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-808)\\nIn his second term's first quarter according to Gallup, Trump's approval rating was 45 percent—somewhat better than his first term, and far below the 60 percent average of other presidents. Support remained polarized; he had the approval of 90 percent among Republicans, 37 percent among independents, and 4 percent among Democrats.[[798]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-809)\\n### Scholarly rankings\\nFurther information: [Historical rankings of presidents of the United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historical_rankings_of_presidents_of_the_United_States \\\"Historical rankings of presidents of the United States\\\")\\nIn [C-SPAN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C-SPAN \\\"C-SPAN\\\")'s 2021 survey of presidential historians,[[799]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-810) [historians ranked Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historical_rankings_of_presidents_of_the_United_States#2021_C-SPAN \\\"Historical rankings of presidents of the United States\\\") as the fourth-worst president. He rated lowest in the leadership characteristics categories for moral authority and administrative skills.[[800]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-811)[[801]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-812) The [Siena College Research Institute](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siena_College_Research_Institute \\\"Siena College Research Institute\\\")'s 2022 survey [ranked him](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historical_rankings_of_presidents_of_the_United_States#2022_Siena_College \\\"Historical rankings of presidents of the United States\\\") third-worst. He was ranked near the bottom in all categories except for luck, willingness to take risks, and party leadership, and ranked last in several categories.[[802]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-scri_22-813) In 2018 and 2024, members of the [American Political Science Association](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Political_Science_Association \\\"American Political Science Association\\\") ranked him the worst president.[[803]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-814)[[804]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-815)\\n## See also\\n  * [List of awards and honors received by Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_awards_and_honors_received_by_Donald_Trump \\\"List of awards and honors received by Donald Trump\\\")\\n  * [Pseudonyms used by Donald Trump](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pseudonyms_used_by_Donald_Trump \\\"Pseudonyms used by Donald Trump\\\")\\n\\n\\n## Notes\\n  1. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-5)** Beginning when Trump was three years old, his father gave each of his children $6,000 every year, the maximum allowed without incurring a gift tax. To avoid taxes, Fred made them landlords of two of his housing developments, paying each $13,928 in rent every year.\\n  2. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-11)** Later, in 2015, he threatened his high school, colleges, and the [College Board](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/College_Board \\\"College Board\\\") with legal action if they released his academic records.[[9]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-10)\\n  3. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-20)** UPenn's 1968 commencement program lists Trump as a recipient of a Bachelor's of Science in Economics and not a recipient of Academic Honors.[[15]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-UPenn-17)\\n  4. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-27)** Later Trump would emulate Peale's _[The Power of Positive Thinking](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Power_of_Positive_Thinking#Synopsis \\\"The Power of Positive Thinking\\\")_ in _[The Art of the Deal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Art_of_the_Deal \\\"The Art of the Deal\\\")_.[[23]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201799-26)\\n  5. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-electoral-college_175-0)** Presidential elections in the U.S. are decided by the [Electoral College](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Electoral_College \\\"United States Electoral College\\\"). Each state names a number of electors equal to its representation in [Congress](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Congress \\\"United States Congress\\\") and (in most states) all electors vote for the winner of their state's popular vote.\\n  6. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-178)** A Republican president combined with Republican control of both chambers of Congress.\\n  7. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-433)** Several sources:[[424]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEPion-BerlinBruneauGoetze2022-430)[[425]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHarvey20223\\\"Unlike_Hitler's_coup_attempt,_Trump_already_held_the_top_of_the_office,_so_he_was_attempting_to_hold_onto_power,_not_seize_it_\\\\(the_precise_term_for_Trump's_intended_action_is_a_'self-coup'_or_'autogolpe'\\\\)._Thus,_Trump_was_able_to_plan_for_the_event_well_in_advance_and_with_much_greater_control,_including_developing_the_legal_arguments_that_could_be_used_to_justify_rejecting_the_election's_results\\\"-431)[[426]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTECasta%C3%B1edaJenks2023246\\\"What_the_United_States_went_through_on_January_6th_was_an_attempt_at_a_self-coup,_where_Trump_would_use_force_to_stay_as_head_of_state_even_if_abandoning_democratic_practices_in_the_U.S._Some_advised_Trump_to_declare_martial_law_to_create_a_state_of_emergency_and_use_that_as_an_excuse_to_stay_in_power\\\"-432)\\n  8. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-Fascist_488-0)** Several sources:[[477]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-Axios_Fascist-484)[[478]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-The_Atlantic_Fascist-485)[[479]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-ABC_News_Vermin-486)[[480]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-487)\\n  9. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-568)** Multiple sources describe the economy inherited by the Trump administration as resilient and strong.[[554]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-562)[[555]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-563)[[556]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-564)[[557]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-565)[[558]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-566)[[559]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-567)\\n  10. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-613)** CBS could find no criminal records for 75% of the Venezuelans detained.[[603]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-612)\\n  11. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-Cult_669-0)** Multiple sources:[[652]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTESundahl2022\\\"\\\\[In\\\\]_a_model_for_distinguishing_between_popularity_and_personality_cults_based_on_three_parameters_covering_a_representational_and_social_practice_dimension..._Trump_and_Putin_belong_in_the_domain_of_personality_cults\\\"-662)[[653]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEFranksHesami2021\\\"Results_of_the_current_study..._may_lend_credence_to_accusations_that_some_Trump_supporters_have_a_cult-like_loyalty_to_the_45th_president\\\"-663)[[654]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEAdams2021256-664)[[655]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEReyes2020869-665)[[656]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDiamond202396\\\"The_cult_of_Trumpism_fosters_and_exploits_paranoia_and_allegiance_to_an_all-powerful,_charismatic_figure,_contributing_to_a_social_milieu_at_risk_for_the_erosion_of_democratic_principles_and_the_rise_of_fascism\\\"-666)[[657]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHassan2024xviii\\\"...Trump_employs_many_of_the_same_techniques_as_prominent_cult_leaders\\\"-667)[[658]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_note-668)\\n\\n\\n## References\\n  1. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201730,_37_1-0)** [Kranish & Fisher 2017](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#CITEREFKranishFisher2017), pp. 30, 37.\\n  2. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher2017v_2-0)** [Kranish & Fisher 2017](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#CITEREFKranishFisher2017), p. v.\\n  3. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-3)** Horowitz, Jason (September 22, 2015). [\\\"Donald Trump's Old Queens Neighborhood Contrasts With the Diverse Area Around It\\\"](https://www.nytimes.com/2015/09/23/us/politics/donald-trumps-old-queens-neighborhood-now-a-melting-pot-was-seen-as-a-cloister.html). _[The New York Times](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times \\\"The New York Times\\\")_. Retrieved November 7, 2018.\\n  4. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBuettnerCraig202430%E2%80%9331_4-0)** [Buettner & Craig 2024](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#CITEREFBuettnerCraig2024), pp. 30–31.\\n  5. **[^](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKranishFisher201733,_38,_45_6-0)** [Kranish & Fisher 2017](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump#CITEREFKranishFisher2017), pp. 33, 38, 45.\\n  6. **[^](https:/